Devices to follow people are increasingly in demand by parents who want to monitor their children.
Fear for their child causes more and more people to think about monitoring them through GPS.
The system, put in place at the beginning to search for the cars, can just as well be used to trace the movement of people and to help in some cases such as forgiveness that of little Anicka Janatkova.
The instrument is currently as large as a box of matches, but will soon be the waist-square.
Little Anicka has moved a large number of people and they have begun to find ways to better protect their children.
Even if the price is to disturb their privacy.
'We are trying to tell everyone that this is not about espionage but only another way to reduce the probability that there will be another tragedy,' says Petr Pilin of MAcro Weil who is one of the suppliers of tracking devices on the Czech market.
'I confess that there may be cases of misuse of such devices, such as putting it in the bags of a person who is not aware, but I have not yet encountered such cases.'
'The majority of people are customers who actually buy the product for safety reasons, adds Pilin.
In his view, children should always be aware for the purposes of "the box" and how it could be used in an emergency.
Child psychologist Dana Bockova draws the attention that if the child doesn't want to wear something of this kind, he'll get boasted.
'And even the little ones can show great creativity when it comes to getting rid of something on purpose when they don't want to be under control.'
'Mobile mobile phones, which can clamp like these tracing devices, have already brought parents to long expectations,' says Bockova.
In her view, the fact that this type of device could help in the event of an attack was highly questionable.
'In the case of a prepared kidnap, the author will make every effort to ensure that the child does not have an electronic appliance that can be located."
"Actually, it's the phone."
'As soon as the tracing apparatus develops, they (the criminals) will also fetch them because the sale will be supported by an advertising campaign.'
'In such cases, criminals will always be ahead of the police,' says the psychologist.
She nevertheless confessed that such devices could help during an unprepared assault by a paedophile.
Robert Cech of RC Monitoring, which offers tracking services for both cars and individuals, claims that having such devices can help, even if it is destroyed in the case of an attack.
"The apparatus can be parameterized in such a way that it is transmitted regularly after a determined period".
"In the event that the appliance is destroyed, there is still the last position of the person concerned."
'Facer devices also have an inclin meter which immediately signals the position of the sliding person and ends up lying without moving,' says Cech.
According to him, so-called Intelligent Mobile Phones which are equipped with a browsing system, can offer a comparable service after formatting it with special software.
The problem of current tracing systems is that it is either a device similar to those wielded by the size of a clamp box or to the smallest telephone devices.
We can always lose them.
Pilina of Macro Weil, this is why tests are currently being carried out to make devices that look similar to common digital watches.
'This will have the advantage that the potential aggressor will not be able to recognise the tracing device.'
'Yeven he'll realize it after a certain period of time, which gave the police a little more time,' he adds.
Regardless of whether the tracing appliance is developed in bracelet or pendulum, it will always use the same technology as for tracing cars.
It is a combination of GPS position acquisition systems (which is commonly used in autonavigation) and a function of a mobile telephone which alerts the current position of a vehicle.
The army has so many detonating ropes around the Czech Republic, but it can hold for only two years.
The army procured 445 kilometre kilometre-operated ropes for the explosives.
Already last year the Ministry of Defence had purchased 224 square kilometers.
As a result, the army has reservations for 225 years.
The problem is that the shelf lifespan is two to a maximum of two to four years.
