Households are going to pay an additional 100,000 kroons per month for the dwelling on a monthly basis.
From January onwards, households will have to pay an additional charge not only for all types of energy but also for water and sewerage, heating Supplies and rent in some places will also increase.
As of January, families will have to shoot their purse up to 100 kroons a month longer than before.
Electricity is increasing by 4.6 per cent, gas by almost 2 per cent more, in some localities you will have to pay up to one-ten-first the water supply and in many places central-heating supplies will also be more expensive.
The increase in electricity is expected to be close to 100 kroons in apartments once more than at present.
However, all suppliers have lowered the prices of electricity in view of the significant development to encourage the manufacture of renewable energy, in particular photovoltaic batteries.
The gas rises moderately but of course at a time when people need it the most to heat and therefore have the greatest consumption.
It can therefore also be counted about a hundred or more kroons.
Although electricity and gas are increasing throughout the Czech Republic, the change in the price of Central Heater differs from city to city.
It is mainly the largest municipalities that plan the increase in prices.
For example, households in Pardubice or Hradec Kraloce from January for central heaven for central heating almost an additional SKK 50 per month, Libérec families will pay more than 1,000 kroons annually.
For the inhabitants of Prague is preparing an increase of 9 per cent for water and sewerage in the event of an increase in infrastructure rent.
If rents do not change, pragiers will pay an additional 6.8 per cent for an additional 6.8 per cent.
Pricing in Porgue will still be among the lowest in comparison with the country as a whole.
In Northern Bohemia, where prices are already among the highest, water and sewerage are increasing by 7.4 per cent.
Rental prices will, for example, significantly increase in the 450 000 rented apartments starting in January, i.e. at the end of regulation.
For example, CPI Byty, which owns almost 2,500 apartments in Usti nad Labem, has decided an increase of up to 100 per cent.
Unaggregated those who did not agree with the further increase in rent would be brought before the company in the courts.
Regulated rent paid by people in nearly 300,000 apartments could increase next year by 10 per cent.
