Two L-159 planes were for the first time in foreign hands in Spain.
Two L-159 planes of the five in total, against which the Ministry of Defence exchanged Casa C-295M transport planes, took off on Tuesday for Seville, Spain, where the aircraft manufacturer Casa-EADS is based.
This is the first time that aircraft of this type have become the property of someone other than the Czech army.
However, they went abroad for demonstrative exercises or flights.
These are two-seat versions of L-159T1 which were until now in service in the Czech Army and were in flight.
The remaining three aircraft are single-seater versions of the L-159.
For the time immersue, they are stationed at Aéro Vodochody and the commercial intermediary - Omnipol - took them into account last week.
According to Jan Pejsek of the Ministry of Defense's Press Department, the physical departure of Vodochody's planes is currently unknown.
'The single-seat L-159 airplanes planned for the exchange have been taken on long-stored fixed-fashioned aircraft without causing additional burdens or investments for the Ministry of Defence.
'The two-seat L-159 T1 have been traded in the state,' said Pravo Pejsek.
He added that the army would not remain without a two-seater since the ministry signed in May 2009 with Aéro Vodochody an agreement worth 200 million kroons for the conversion of four unused L-159 single-seats into two two-seats.
The army took delivery of the first aircraft in July, the second planned for delivery at the end of this year.
According to Pejsek EADS is committed to introducing aircraft into the equipment of certain Alliance countries.
'Continued in the contract it is clearly stated that the person taking L-159 aircraft into account is EADS and that they will be used to train pilots from NATO member countries.'
'It is, therefore, the one that takes into account how to carry this out,' said Pejsek.
Hospodarske noviny announced some time ago that the Spanish army was not interested in L-159.
The exchange of five L-159s for a Casa aircraft and the purchase of three other 3.5 billion kroner aircraft was endorsed last year by the government of Mirek Topomanek.
The Ministry of the Department of the Department of Defense did not issue a competitive bidding process and contacted EADS directly.
At the same time, the Directorate of Defence argued that the exchange ensured a future export of L-159 abroad.
The Czech army purchased 72 aircraft from Aéro Vodochody and uses only 24, wanting to sell the rest.
Purchase of Casa aircraft is likely to have legal implications.
the European Commission, however, has lodged a complaint against the Czech Ministry of Defence with the European Court of Justice because the planes should have been chosen as part of a Europe-wide call for tenders and in no way by contacting a manufacturer directly.
The army has already taken delivery of three Casa planes, the latter is scheduled to arrive at Prague Kbely airport in two weeks.
Older index is an indicator of low cancer risk
Men who have an index older than the annulus are at a lower risk of having prostatic cancer.
This is the surprising conclusion of a study published on Wednesday by the British magazine Cancer.
This may depend on the level of the male hormone testosterone.
'Our conclusions show that the relative length of the knitter serves as a simple test of the risk of prostate cancer, especially for men before the age of 'co-writer Ros Eeles, co-author Ros Eeles, Professor at the Institute for Research on Cancer at the University of Warwick.
This could help to determine which men should undergo the prostate cancer test, in particular by Combining the Genetic Risk or Other Agreeingal Factors such as Family Health Bridging, Eeles added.
During the period of 1994-2009 she followed with colleagues in Great Britain more than 1,500 patients with pro-proscine cancer, at the same time nearly 3,000 healthy men.
More than half of the participants in the study had the right-hand index shorter than the annulus.
For them, as for 19 per cent of those with their fingers of the same length, we found the same probability of prostate-catering cancer.
However, in the case of those who had an index older than the annular, the risk fell to 33 per cent for men before the age of 60, the reduction in risk reached 87 per cent.
The length ratio of the mentioned two fingers - formed during the prenatal period - indicates, inter alia, different levels of sexual hormones to which the child is exposed in the womb.
Less testosterone corresponds to a longer index.
Previous research had shown that testosterone helped the development of pro-muncient prostate.
Father Vacla Paces, a renowned Czech geneticist and former president of the Academy of Sciences, said for CTK that he is aware of this study but it does not seem to him that the relationship between finger lengths and cancer is "completely demonstrated".
