The Americans Have a New 'Smart Weapon'
The U.S. Army sent a new "intelligent" weapon to Afghanistan, known only as XM-25.
The generals believe that this new automatic rifle will have an influence on the conflict there with the Taliban and 'make the game' for their own benefit.
The new bulwark is like a super-precise cannon
Its projectiles are laser-guided with an accuracy of a few meters.
Special 25-gauge mammals developed for this rifle and containing a highly shattered explosive in fact operate like small grenades.
Munitions can be programmed in such a way that they will detonate in contact with the target but also at any point of their ballosis trajectory.
It can therefore be used against an enemy lurking behind an obstacle, a wall or a drift.
Nor is it protected from above, and when it falls on a deadly rain of steel splinters, it can put him out of combat as well.
According to Richard Audette who took part in the development of the XM-25, the new rifle is the first infantry weapon using "intelligent" technology.
'Soldier uses it essentially in such a way that he finds the target, sends a laser ray that determines the distance, thus obtains a precise aiming point, according to which it corrects its site sight and presses the trigger.
"Let's say you're aiming at 543 meters."
"When you hurry the trigger, the weapon loads and shoots 543 rms plus or minus 1, 2.3 meters," said Audette.
Exploited 400 jobs
The Fujitsu Group relocated its subsidiary from Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
Next year in July, 400 collaborators will be moving to Astro-Park their place of work, which is currently located in the office building in the Bad Homburg area.
The Japanese group Fujitsu relocated its Bad Homburg branch to Frankfurt.
As the giant specializing in computer-manufacturing and maintenance next year said next year in July, the place of work of 400 men and women, located so far in the Marienbader Square in Bad Homburg, will be relocated to Astro Park, a huge office complex located on the street of the Niederrad Quarter in Frankfurt.
A spokeswoman for Fujitsu explained that, in the medium-term term, the group is expected to bring together an additional 100 jobs still scattered throughout the region.
Rental offer has been extremely attractive, and Astro Park's open-area offices are better suited to the working mode of the collaborators.
In Bad Homburg as well, Fujitsu was just tenant.
Managing Director Bernd Wagner said: 'Frankfurt-Niederrad is an important location for us with a remarkable infrastructure.
He claimed that connection to the airport was of great importance for a global company such as Fujitsu.
The new relocation of an important company inside the region yesterday aroused the devotion of the winners and caused the disappointment of the losers.
The Head of Economic Affairs of the Municipality of Frankfurt, Markus Frank (CDU), stated that, once again, an "planetary actor" had been conquered.
It is an "extraordinary profession of faith relative to location".
Fujitsu is suitable for the city and its settlement is an important boost for Niederrad.
The Mayor of Bad Homburg, Michael Korwisi (The Greens), regretted Fujitsu's decision.
At an early stage, the management informed the municipality of this intention.
'It goes without saying that we have offered our support to this endeavour,' said Mr Korwisi.
However, Friendly relations could not have changed the difficulties of the computer manufacturer vis-à-vis the owner of Marienbader Square building.
At the end of the day, economic reasons would have been decisive.
It is now a question of preventing such an exceptional location from remaining unoccupied for a long time.
"I have the hope that the landowner and lessor of the building will be co-cooperative."
Ten years ago, the Town Hall Square building, used so far by the Fujitsu company and the "small Vertical", as well as the rest of Marienbad Square buildings, were part of the controversial spa building project.
Resulting from a joint project of the municipality and a private project developer, a re-entection of the city was created between the railway station and the Town Hall, which aroused many critics, not only because of the impressive layout of the areas that were formerly used as a car park, but also because of the height of the buildings.
In 2001, Rating and Investment Consulting Company Feri, the manufacturer of computers for travel distribution systems, Start Amadeus, and Fujitsu, a computer manufacturer still associated with Siemens, moved into buildings.
In 2006, the German Fragmentian Immobilien-Chancenn group bought the buildings occupied by Fujitsu at the same time as the real-estate Morgan Stanley real-estavity fund. One year later, these buildings were resold to the Spanish company Falcon Real Estate Investments SA based in Madrid.
The Fujitsu group headquartered in Tokyo as the third largest provider of computer products in the world.
For example, it makes computers and servers available to businesses and also provides the essential service.
Its branch established in the Rhine-Main region is one of the most important in Germany: Fujitsu certainly has many more employees in Munich.
Fujitsu Technology Solutions leaves Bad Homburg to move to Frankfurt-Niederrad; the other subsidiaries present are Fujitsu Services and a company named TDS.
For this purpose, 7,000 square metearts were rented in the Astro Park building.
This twenty-year-old complex, whose name is reminiscent of its star-shaped plan, houses, inter alia, the headquarters of the Confederation of the Electronic and Electrotechnical Industry.
