Luciano Ferrari master glacier first explained to the participants the ideal characteristics of perfect ice.
Determining not only good flavor, but also silky suppleness and consistency.
However, Luciano doesn't misregard during forming the cherry on the cake of the national superstructure and, in the general south: 'Italian ice cream is simply the best: and then it still retains a lot of romanticism.'
The first step in manufacturing seems quite simple.
Luciano pours the basic ingredients, such as milk, cream, milk powder, sugar and stabilizers into a large mixer that heats everything at 85 degrees thanks to an electronic program, then immediately cools to four degrees.
It then opens a faucet installed on the machine: it emerges a pasteurized basic white cream, which is the basis of all kinds of milk ice cream.
The train-former is immediately attacked: Is it possible to make ice using only natural substances without a stabilizer?
How can we unleash the personal pleasure of the experiment?
Mr. Luciano cautioned against being misleaded by the spirit of the times.
Anyone who only talks about ice cream "manufactured from the natural ingredients", may yet have used stabilizers: most often a natural product, e.g. guar seed flour obtained from guar beans.
It is obviously possible to manufacture ice without using a stabilizer, only it must be sold within a period of 24 o'clock, otherwise it collapses and the customer will chew nothing other than ice-cream granules.
Certain changes in the basic mass are permitted.
It becomes more fascinating with flavors.
Master Luciano establishes the difference between the ingredients that one acquires in the fruit or confectionery radius of the supermarket.
In addition, there are powders and pastes.
"Suppliers will all try to pass on dozens of aromatid ingredients to you."
"However, you should just try."
Let's be in fruit sorbet recipes with sugar and fresh fruit.
Either in ice creams made from the white base mass which, by adding egg, becomes a yellow cream and, when cocoa powder is added, forms the basis of all possible dess desserts containing chocolate.
Now, all participants in the course must move to the use of ice-cream machines.
Luciano invites them from time from time to time to make four kinds of ice cream: vanilla, Chocolate, moka and hazelnut with or without egg in the staple, with more or less vanilla flavor or other natural ingredients.
The different kinds of cream are also subjected to the same experience.
More aromatic substances, more fruit purées: from the point of view of taste, this is not absolutely better.
Hazelnut ice cream does not necessarily have the walnut taste when it consists largely of hazelnuts.
Kiwi ice cream, which is very popular, is made from the group using more fruit, less water and a little less stabilizer.
'Liquind a balanced mixture correctly highlights the taste of an ice cream,' says Luciano.
Ice manufacturing is a computational exercise: if peach peach purée with a lot of sugar is used, the amount of sugar must be reduced in the basic recipe.
An additional sugar is needed for lemon ice cream.
Luciano presents an appliance with which it determines the sugar content of the different types of fruit (binders use it to measure Oechsle in grape must).
Happily, the tables facilitate the calculations.
The manufacture of ice no longer requires, as in the long term, physical intervention.
It isn't just the machine for automatically manufacturing the base mass that helps.
There is also an embodiment in which the available ice mixture is simultaneously beaten to make it unctuous and brought back to negative temperatures.
In spite of all automations, slight failures are not excluded.
Brazilian Gustavo fuzzy with fruit mammal from his head to toe and thus shudder his fellow studies when the control of the colossal mixing bowl espoils him.
Master Luciano is of the opinion that 'such an incident is likely to occur in each course: it is only a matter of time.'
