But they should make them public.
Kuiper concedes that he works for the United States-based International Life Sciences Institute.
This body sees itself as a neutral forum bringing together experts from, inter alia, in science, industry and policy in order to assess the risks of biotechnology without the pay of a lobbies.
In this case, independent experts such as Mr Then disapprove of the fact that genetically modified plants are accepted without any animal feeding tests and that the impact of these plants on the environment has not been tested.
'We would like to know, for example, the incidences of genetically modified maize caused to the field,' says Mr Then.
The current practice consists in testing only isolated proteins and not the entire genetically modified plant, which does not make it possible to obtain reliable results.
'If we don't look for the risks, how then can we discover them?" asks Mr. Then about an accusatory tone.
This is also the view of Mrs Angelika Hilbeck.
This scientist has been carrying out genetic research since 1994 and is currently working at the Poly-Technical School in zurich.
In this region as well as in the United States, now also in Europe, the industrial sector is more and more trying to influence or discredit criticical scientists.
As a result, there are still barely researchers who are not linked to companies.
In the views of companies, critical studies would not be published.
Mrs Hilbeck stated that 'the industry monitors safety research.
