However, in June 2008, an IAEA expert team led by former chief inspector Oli Heinonen uncircumstances in el-Kibar were uncircumcised traces of man-made uranium.
In addition, the United Nations High Commissioner for the Advancement of the Republic of the International Criminal Court, the United States of America, received satellite images of commercial providers showing the direct follow-up of the attack, and it seems that the latter speak loving more eloquently than those presented by the CIA.
However, these photographs are not available on the market because the anonymous sponsor, perhaps Israel, acquired them on an exclusive basis.
As such, it is not possible for the IAEA to establish their authenticity in an objective manner.
Syria had always assured that it had never carried out a secret atomic programme and that it did not build a reactor.
Damas maintains that the uranium found on the site comes from the weapons used by Israel.
However, the IAEA resubmitted this argument after the particle analysis described as implausible.
