Democratic Routine
Support for democracy in Latin America continues to increase, just like that of private enterprise.
Crime has become a greater concern than unemployment.
And Brazil is seen as more influential than the United States in a large part of the region.
These are some of the conclusions of the latest Latino-Baretro survey taken in 18 countries and published exclusively by The Economist.
Given that the survey has been carried out on a regular basis since 1995, it monitors attitudinal changes in the region.
Two linked elements emerge from the results of this year's survey carried out in September and the beginning of October.
The first is the rather sluggish humour of Latin America.
The latter is the growing stability of attitudes towards democracy and its main institutions.
Support for democracy has increased significantly in a number of countries around South American Pacific (see table 1).
For example, in Peru, where economic growth has reached an average of 6% a year since 2002, support for democracy increased from a minimum of 40 per cent in 2005 to 61 per cent this year.
It has also increased in Mexico, where the economy picked up following a sharp drop in output last year.
But only about half of the respondents in Mexico and Brazil, the region’s two most populous democrats, are convinced Democrats, reducing the regional average (see Table 2).
Some 4 per cent of respondents in the region expressed satisfaction with the way democracy in their countries functioned in practice; this is the same figure as last year but with a sharp increase over the 25 per cent in 2001.
Some 34% said that they had confidence in the Parliament of their country and 45 per cent in their government, the same as last year in both cases, but a sharp increase compared to 2003 (17% and 24 per cent, respectively).
Ten years ago, economic problems in Latin America shackled support for democracy.
While this stood firm during the global financial crisis, it is certainly due to the fact that many countries in the United States have experienced only a brief recession and benefited from a strong recovery.
In addition, a social security network has helped to protect the poor.
(The United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America reported this week that the proportion of Latin Americans living in poverty increased only marginally in 2009 and declined again this year to 32%.)
But the mood varies considerably from one country to another (see Chart 4).
The good economic performance of Brazil and the popularity of its outgoing president, Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, translates into a widely shared sense of progress
What is more rare in Mexico and Central America, where the recession has been more severe and violent crimes have escalated.
This is only for the second time since the poll started that more respondents across Latin America as a whole cited crime rather than unemployment as the most important problem for their country (see table 5).
About 31 per cent say that they or one of their loved ones have been subjected to a criminal offence in the previous year; however, this figure is down from 38% last year and is the lowest since 1995.
A more robust economic performance is reflected in the increase in support of private enterprise and the market economy: around 71% of respondents in the region say that private enterprise is indispensable for development, compared with 56% in 2007 (see Chart 6).
Some 5 per cent believe that the market economy is the only system that will return their developed country, compared to 47% last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela take this view (and 80% support the private enterprise); although their left-wing president Hugo Chavez expropriates many private companies.
Brazil is the greatest leader in the region, mentioned by 19% of respondents (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, unchanged compared to last year) and Venezuela (9%, down 11% since last year).
However, the United States is considered to be the most influential country by respondents in Mexico and most of Central America, while the United States enjoys this distinction in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
