The largely overheated economy is likely to grow by almost 9% this year.
The official inflation rate now stands at 11.1 per cent, but many private estimates and wage demands by the trade unions are close to 30 per cent.
Mr. Kirchner, who took office in 2003 when Argentina feared an economic melt-down, had given priority to growth and poverty reduction rather than controlling inflation.
It refused to deal with the IMF, harassed the private sector and purged INDEC, the statistical office, forgery of official inflated statistics.
Is Mrs Fernandez now moderate these policies?
It called for talks between private companies and trade union leaders.
And his civil servants say that they want to limit salary increases next to 18%.
The economy has been stimulated by high world prices for agricultural exports from Argentina.
But inflation eroded the boost of growth following a strong devaluation in 2002.
Perhaps that is the reason why she shows signs of re-establishing Argentina's financial relations to the rest of the world.
This year, the government entered into an agreement with bondholders who had refused a previous forced restructuring of the debt for which Argentina had not settled its maturities in 2001.
"Noxidon Ms Fernandez seems to be edging towards an agreement with creditor governments grouped in the Paris Club, to which it it owes some '6.7 trillion".
The Kirchners have always insisted that this could not involve supervision by the IMF, as is customary.
But last month Amado Boudou, the Minister of the Economy, announced that the Fund would provide "technical assistance" to design a new national consumer price index, which resembles a prelude to INDEC's clean-up.
It is not yet clear whether Mrs Fernandez wants to remain in politics beyond the end of her term of office now that her husband is no longer by her side.
If it does so, it needs to gain support from the middle-class, among which kirchnerism is highly advocated.
When the Argentinians return from their Summer Holiday in March, the wave of Sympain for it will have resurfaced.
If she wants to win another election, she will have to decide whether or not to repudiate many of her husband’s policies.
