Temecula Mosque project is approved by the Planning Commission.
A Mosque project in Temecula received unanimous support from the local planning commission after more than five hours of public testimony, which was often passionate.
Criticism attacked the project by claiming that it was promoting both terrorism and traffic junkitting.
But the commissioners ruled on Wednesday evening that the new religious center respected local laws and that it would be aesthetically impressive.
'Many good people came to support it: Christians, Jews, Baha'is you mention it,' said Hadi Nael, President of the Temecula Valley Islamic Centre, speaking on Thursday morning.
"I'm proud of it as an American citizen."
"That shows that justice prevails."
'Opportunity is really small,' he added.
"They just have very loud voices."
"We have good people in this valley."
The sitting began at 6 p.m. and lasted until almost midnight with, most of the time, people pronunciating themselves for or against the centre, at roughly equally distributed speaking time, according to the Press-Enterprise.
The debate has continued on the website of the newspaper.
"There is no more America," one speaker wrote.
"It's another monkey planet with Miss Liberty dead in the stove."
"America fell."
Planned Islamic centres have also provoked protests in other parts of the country, such as the Ground zero, the site of the attacks of 11 September 2001 in New York City.
Imam Mahmoud Harmoush denunciated violence in Temecula Valley.
Its congregation of the order of 135 families coexisted without incidents in Riverside County for years, but it wanted a greater and better space than the warehouse it rented on a shared property with a manufacturer of water-filtration systems.
The new 24.9-43-square-foot mosque with Mediterranean-style architecture would consist of two minarets and would rise 43 feet high.
It would occupy a site of 4.32 hectares on the Nicholas road about two miles east of Chaparral Lyceum.
Opponents can still appeal against the municipal council against this project.
Arsenic-tolerant microbe raises the possibility of new types of lives on Earth, or elsewhere.
Discovering a strange bacterium, which can use arsenic as one of its nutritious elements, enlarges the field to find new forms of life on Earth and perhaps beyond.
Although the researchers uncovered the unusual bacter bacterium on Earth, they say it shows that life has possibilities beyond the major elements that have been considered essential.
"This organism has a dual capacity."
'It can grow either with phosphorus or with ingslides."
"That's why it's very special, even though it's far from being a truly 'forensive,' commented Paul Davies C. W. of the Arizona State University, co-perpetrator of the report featured on Thursday's online edition of Science.
Six major elements have long been identified as essential for life: carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur.
But the researchers found that the bacterium, discovered in Lake Mono, California, is able to further develop after replacing phosphorus with ingslides.
'We're wondering what's still possible,' said Ariel Anbar D. of Arizona State University, co-author of the report.
Discovery is important in researching life beyond the Earth because researchers need to be able to recognize life, to know what life looks like, says Anbar.
The study focuses on a microbe found on Earth.
However, the announcement of a press conference to discuss it, which did not divulge the details of the discovery, generated widespread speculation on the Internet that the report might divulge the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
This has not been the case.
"Securates that, in other planetary environments, organisms may be able to use other elements to activate biochemistry and that the "Standard" set of elements, which we deem absolutely necessary for life, may not be fixed,' commented Charles Cockell, Professor at the Institute for Planetary and Space Scientific Research, Open University, Milton Keynes, United Kingdom.
Cockell wasn't part of the research team.
'This work is new because it shows the substitutation of one element to another in fundamental biochemistry and biochemical structure,' added Cockell.
It wasn't an accidental discovery.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of the NASA Institute of Astrology, who led the study, chose Lake Mono because it has high levels of arsenic.
Arsecenes and phosphorus are chemically similar; for example, it speculated that a microbe prone to the two substances might be substituting for each other.
'Arsecendy is toxic mainly because its chemical behaviour is really similar to that of phosphorus.'
'As a result, it is difficult for agencies to distinguish these elements separately.'
'But Arsecenide is different enough that it doesn't work exactly like phosphorus; it penetrates into the interior and, in a way, grazes the work of our biochemical machine,' explained Anbar.
The researchers collected the bacterium known as GFAJ-1 and exposing it to increasing concentrations of arsenic to adapt and grow.
The microbe grows better with phosphorus but the fact that it can live with icscenario instead raises the possibility that an edarsenic-like form of life may occur naturally, either elsewhere on Earth or on another planet or moon where arsenic is more common.
Jamie S. Foster, Assistant Professor of Microbiology at the University of Florida, said that the idea that arsenic could be replaced by phosphorus, was not new; but nothing ever showed that it could work.
Arsecenes was more common at the beginning of the existence of the Earth, she said; so the researchers hypothesized that the first forms of life could have used it.
This suggests that there might be other ways of forming life, not just the way in which life on Earth was formed at the beginning", said Foster, who was not part of Wolfe-Simon's research team.
The research was funded by NASA, the Ministry of Energy and the National Health Institute.
The last two stowed exposure dogs were found in Compton
The other two exhibition dogs on four spots from the exteripher of a motel at Bellflower were spotted, the Los Angeles County sheriff analyzed it on Thursday.
The four exhibition dogs – two akitas and two Welsh Pembroke corgis – disappeared on Tuesday when someone stole the Chevrolet Express van in which they spent the night outside a Bellflower motel 6, the authorities said.
Two of the Dogs were retrieved on Wednesday afternoon and the other two were found in late Night on Wednesday, the spokeswoman for Los Angeles County Sher's Sheriff's Department told Steve Whitmore.
'The good piece of good news is that the dogs are fine,' said Whitmore.
"They are in good forms."
"They were returned to their righteous owners."
Whitmore refused to tell exactly where the dogs had been found, in what neighborhood and what were the circumstances of their discovery.
"An investigation is ongoing."
"We don't want to say it," Whitmore said.
"We're following orders."
Dog owners Kristina Rickard and David Peek couldn't be reached to provide a comment on Thursday.
They were reunited on Wednesday afternoon with their two Akitas at an animal shelter in Downey County.
Sheriff's deputies restained the van and the two dogs separately in Compton.
The Akitas - Trace, a 3-year-old male, and Didi, a 10-month-old feminine - were unharmed, the authorities said.
But Bunny and Peter, the corgis that Rickard and Peek had planned to show in regional competitions this week, on behalf of the owners of the Japanese Dogs, were missing until their discovery on Wednesday evening.
Peek and Rickard, who live in Redding, are in town this weekend as part of Long Beach's national "AKC/Eukanuba" national champion, where they intended to present Trace.
The other dogs were expected to take part in the competitions of other events expected in the days leading to the "AKC/Eukanuba" Exhibition, Peek told the Times on Wednesday.
Southern California is one of the stops behind the couple's almost a month-long canine exhibition tour.
On Monday evening, Peek and Rickard checked the motel.
They often stay in motels and park their vans outside, right next to their bedroom window.
They took three other dogs – a Boxer puppy, a Parson Russell burrow and a Breton wielder – who stayed with them in the motel room.
But they left Akitas and corgis with a thicker fur, in the van where it would be cooler.
They opened the windows a little bit, so that the dogs could have fresh air.
Peek and Rickard reported flying the van at 6 a.m. on Tuesday.
Peek said that they would offer a reward to anyone who could take them to corgis with an implanted microchip to identify them.
It is not clear whether a reward was awarded on Thursday.
The lion is ready to blush again, the restructuring of MGM no longer awaits the final blurry of the yard
After turbulent 18 months, MGM finally got the go-a-go-tamper to start from scratch.
New York Federal Bankruptilities Justice officially leased on Monday the “pre-packaged” plan by the creditors, according to which about USD 5 billion of debt would be erased; paving the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company to concentrate its efforts on the production and distribution of movies and television shows.
With Thursday’s decision that the plan meets the requirements of the United States Bankrupt-Failure Code, MGM no longer remains legal hurdles for getting back on the whistle-boughts of Entertainment Spyglass, Gary Barber and Roger Bornbaum, who will become the managing heads of what will be a leadered studio.
The staff will be reduced to approximately 320 out of 400 at the outset.
MGM will not actually emerge from Chapter 11 until JPMorgan Chase enters into a USD 500 million credit facility planned to finance GMM's new operations and productions.
It is currently being traded under licence by the investment bank, which faces USD 75 million, and is expected to be funded by mid-December.
The process of reorganizing the studio began in May 2009 when the investment bank Moelis and Co. was engaged to advise management on how to invest its finances, since it did not have the means to pay interest amounting to USD 300 million per year.
In the course of August, general manager Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former CEO of Enron, and a recovery expert known for his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Attempts by Moelis and Cooper to resolve MGM’s financial difficulties and satisfy the more than 140 creditors of the studio took much longer than expected.
After several months of discussions, at the end of 2009 the parties agreed to put the company on sale.
However, the auction attracted a small number of bidders, none of whom were prepared to pay the sum of an additional 2 billion whose creditors were researched.
In the spring of this year, it had to be done again when the MGM team began work on a restructuring plan which, after months of internal disagreements and debates, was finally approved by the creditors at the end of October.
When MGM officially emerges from bankruptcy in the next few weeks, Moelis and Cooper received multi-million-dollar bonuses for their work.
Moelis and Co., which has already earned 1.2 million United States dollars, will receive a payment of 9.5 million United States dollars.
Cooper companies, which have raised EUR 375 000 per month for their management services of the company, will receive a “succeed bonus” of USD 4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum intends to relaunch the production of MGM, which released only one film this year, and set up an annual list of seven or eight films to be released as of 2012.
They also intend to co-finance two films based on "The Hobbit," with Warner B.
The Studio had to make a separate licenship between 265,000,000 and 275 million to fund its share in these two films, to be directed by Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who owns about 18% of MGM's debt, attempted to merge the Studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, where he is the largest shareholder.
However, the recent discussions between the two companies did not lead to an agreement.
