Public prosecutors won't incarcerate Senator Ensign
Ministry of Justice prosecutors raised criminal charges against Senator John Ensign, arising from a case related to an assistant in an old-fasheekerioned campaign and his efforts to obtain lobrnaty work for the woman's husband, haunted the senator's lawyers on Wednesday.
The decision puts an end to the most serious legal threat faced by Ensign, the Nevada Republican who was regarded as a possible republican presidential candidate in 2012 until the scandal surrounding his case made national headlines last year and harmed his career.
Mr. Ensign's office stated that it was "very happy" by the decision as a result of a one-year investigation.
The Senator could still be exposed to disciplinary action taken by the Senate Ethics Committee.
Ensign's lawyers stated that the Ministry of Justice had "informed that Senator Ensign was no longer the subject of the investigation and that he did not intend any charges against him in this case."
The Ministry of Justice had refrained from commenting in that regard.
The advertisement came as a kind of surprise for many non-Misstrutorial lawyers outside the Department of the Department of Justice, according to which there would be significant evidence that Ensign conspired to violate a federal criminal ban on lobbies on lobbies.
Interviews, e-mails and other documents, first released in October 2009 in The New York Times, indicated that Mr. Ensign had assisted Douglas Hampton, who was one of his assistants, in securing lucrative lobbying to win the silence of Mr. Hampton and his wife, Cynthia, about the senator's affair with her.
Ms. Hampton once served as Treasurer in an Ensign campaign.
Mr. Ensign contacted a number of political funders to find a job for Mr. Hampton and then defended their rights after hiring Mr. Hampton to lobbie them, in accordance with documents and interviews.
Since Mr. Hampton had recently been a collaborator of Mr. Ensign, he was prohibited for a one-year law from lobbiing his former employer or the Senate as a whole.
Ethics experts argued that the unusual agreement between Mr Ensign and Mr Hampton, who were close friends before the case, appears to be at odds with the so-called revolving door ban on lobbies.
According to the evidence that has been made public, "there seems to be prima facie evidence of a conspiracy to breeze and substantial violation of the Rotary Gate Act", said Stanley Brand, a Washington lawyer who specializes in the defense of clients of a government investigation.
Although the ministry judge refused to explain the reasoning that led to the Ensign decision, Mr. Brand and other ethics lawyers said that the department appeared to be wary of political proceedings after a number of high-level setbacks by its Public Integrity Section, including last year's decisions to drop the charges against former Senator Ted Stevens accused of misconduct.
Federal prosecutors are clearly afraid to take up arms, said Melanie Sloan, Citizen's Director-in-Chief for Accountability and Ethics in Washington, D.C., an advocacy group that received several complaints against Ensign in the case.
'Helean says high-ranking government officials can now assume the laws don't apply to them, she added.
Paul Coggins, Mr. Ensign's lead lawyer, stated in an interview that he had received a telephone call on Monday from a Ministry of Justice prosecutor and a follow-up the next day, telling him that the Ministry was not looking for charges against his client.
Ensign had cooperated with the prosecutors in an attempt to persuad them that Senator, as his office reasserted on Wednesday in a press release, "struggled in accordance with the law."
Mr. Coggins refused to clarify the legal arguments he had made to the Public Prosecutor's Office.
'I am uncomfortable to go into the details of my discussions with the Ministry of Justice, except to say that we think they made the right decision,' he said.
Last month, the Federal Elections Commission decided to abandon an inquest following an independent complaint by the ethics group, arguing that Ensign's parents of EUR 96,000 violated the Federal Electoral Law.
The electoral commission stated that it did not have sufficient evidence to prove that the money was anything but a gift.
NV Energy, a major Nevada company that has long supported the senator, is a company for which Mr. Ensign helped Hampton find lobbying work.
An e-mail sent in December 2008 showed that, after the company welcomed Mr Hampton as a lobbyist, Ensign Chief of Staff John Lopez communicated with Mr Hampton and told him that he was 'marchaising' the Ministry of the Interior on an issue in dispute.
Mr. Ensign also participated in a Lunch with Mr. Hampton and another Nevada Customer, Allegiantying Air, and asked the Transportation Secretary to set a meeting on behalf of the airline after urging Mr. Hampton to lobbiege.
When the news of the case arose last year, Mr. Ensign apologised to his constituents and resigned as a Republican leader in the Senate.
After maintaining a low profile for several months, Mr. Ensign stated two weeks ago that he intended to run for a third term in the Senate.
Annountitulated that Mr. Ensign was no longer the target of a criminal investigation, his Spokeswoman Jennifer Cooper stated that the Senator hoped that the Ethics Committee would quickly follow the example of the Department of Justice and put an end to its investigation.
Senator Ensign looks forward to continuing his hard work on behalf of Nevada people, she said.
Cheshire's Call Will Switch to Tweeting
Half-twelree of them were typing far into the rows in the courtroom.
Every minute they sent reports of horrific testimonies via Twitter.
At the very end, the defence stated that there were more than 14,10,000 messages about the trial of the rape case and children tied to their bed in a house that was set on fire.
On Thursday, the trial largely followed by Steven J. Hayes, who was convicted of the murder of three members of the Cheshire family in Connecticut, should result in an official ending in which the judge would impose the death penalty voted by the jury.
But Mr. Hayes’s lawyers have already filed evidence in court that outline arguments of an appeal, which could occupy the courts for years.
What's provoking is that the intense reports about the lawsuit - as well as the widespread and extensive use by Twitter journalists - created a "circus atmosphere", such widespread and simplistic saturation with incendiary details that the junkit was poorly influenced by the passions of the audience.
The request could prompt appellate judges to discuss whether new technologies require new rules in courtrooms.
'I've come to an experience in this trial; I think there's a real danger that the introduction of new technologies in the courtroom violates human rights in a fair trial,' said Thomas J. Ullmann, lawyer of Mr. Hayes, Chief Lawyer.
The trial judge, Jon C. Bleue of the Superior State Superior Court, laconically rejected the claims in the judgement handed down last week, stating that the media were “carefully controlled” and that there was no evidence that the jury was animated by passion.
But that doesn't seem to be the last word on the issue.
Appellate courts for decades recognize that media coverage of notorious cases can raise questions about the fairness of a trial.
In a landmark 1966 trial, the United States Supreme Court quashed the conviction of an Osteopath of Cleveland, Dr. Sam Sheppard, for the murder of his wife, arguing in his decision that there was a "carnival atmosphere at the trial" because of the media.
The crime is considered by some to have been the inspiration for the TV show and the film "The Fugitive".
Some lawyers have stated that the appellate courts that are seized to review Mr. Hayes's conviction half a century later may be curious given the argument that new rules might be necessary to manage the media in courtrooms, unimaginable at the time of Dr. Sheppard case.
"there is a general reason to be concerned about the impact of the new media" in large-scale lawsuits, said John J. Donohue III, a professor at Stanford Law School who studied the death sentence in Connecticut and wrote a report according to which it was arbitrarily applied.
Prof. Donohue argues that some appellate judges could be intrigued by the idea that limiting instantaneous communication from courtrooms could lead to fairer trials.
The case of tweeting is only one of many others described by Mr. Hayes's lawyers, which can be discussed for years, which delays the execution.
The defence lawyers also state, for example, that Mr. Hayes allegedly suffered harm when Judge Blue refused to allow them to test the reactions of potential jurors by showing them macabricated photographs of criminal scenes during the selection of the jury.
Some jurors claimed to have been horrified by photos of the victims Jennifer Hawke-Petit, aged 48, and her daughters, Hayley, aged 17, and Michaela, aged 11.
Defensive lawyers claim that they will also challenge the constitutionality of the death sentence and will argue that the judge unfairly expelled one of the jurors, who had been critical before the prosecutors, and improperly put pressure on a verdict by making the unusual decision to induce the jury to deliberate within a weekend.
The jurors were not kidnapped.
But their arguments on the effect of intense media coverage of the lawsuit could be the greatest source of interest.
They noted in a protest against the verdict, filed last month, that the case was dominating the Media Scene in Connecticut and that it was being discussed during the election campaign by candidates for Governor and the United States Senate.
They maintained the fact that the computer media continued to stifle the flames of emotion of the case.
"Daily hustle and hurry media tweets, cameras and journalists in front of the courthouse," the lawyers wrote, "didn't look like anything that had previously been seen here in New Haven, and maybe even nationally."
Their emphasis on the coverage of the twitter system is controversial.
In the interviews, some reporters stated that the statement that snapshot news was more incendiary than more staggered communiqués was a luddistic view that would impovermoderise the Court's policy governing media coverage of future trials.
But some lawyers have stated that courts of appeal are often attracted by the arguments that social and technological changes require adaptation by the courts.
Mr. Hayes's lawyers insisted on this claim.
In their file, they refer to the decision of the Supreme Court of 1966 which overthrew Dr. Sheppard's conviction due to media coverage of the trial and clarified that they would invoke the need for new rules for the new media.
"That case," they wrote, "represents that of Sheppard v. Maxwell of the 21st century."
Can civility bring cleavages closer together?
After an invective and negativity campaign, a minimum of civility erupted in D.C. this week.
Was it a harbinding sign of genuine bipartisan cooperation or an illusory moment before the rapid return to the partisan struggle in the capital?
Tuesday’s meeting between President Obama and congressional leaders produced no breakthroughs on the major issues that need to be addressed during the “lidey duck” session.
Evidently however, there was at least a polite discussion about the differences between the two sides as well as the recognition of their shared Responsibilities to govern, rather than another episode in which each side wanted to stagnate points.
Wednesday's public meeting of the Committee on the President's Deficit was equally civilised, in spite of the remaining political divence between members.
It seems clear that the close interaction of several months has created mutual respect that transcends the ideological gap, but which, however, still divides members.
It is hardly sufficient to declare a new era of cooperation.
Both sides are now in a period of mutual testing for signs of co-cooperation and jeopardy, while at the same time trying to assess the limits of public opinion and the patience of voters for a renewed partisan war.
As a result, Tuesday's reunion and subsequent comments from the participants unveiled a minimum of demands or charges.
Philosophical differences between Obama and republicans are too large and too profound to suggest a rapid climate change that has existed over the past two years.
Whether taxation, expenditure, health-care or rights, both parties remain within a distance.
Yet the November election moderated Washington, at least in the short term.
"There is no doubt that things have changed in the tone," said Pete Wehner, an official of the Bush administration, now next to the Center for Ethical and Public Policy.
"This is inevitable after the elections.
Ancient elections aroused partisan passions and then fade away.
It's okay."
On some issues, such as those on the agenda of "lauding duck", Republicans and democrats had no choice but to find a solution.
Bush’s tax cuts will expire at the end of the year, and no one is in favor.
Both sides want to find a way to preserve some or all, on a permanent or temporary basis.
Similarly, a compromise is conceivable on the extension of unemployment benefits, not least because the Republicans cannot afford to give the impression that they are in favour of tax cuts for the rich, while at the same time denying unemployment benefits to unemployed workers.
After Tuesday's meeting in the White House, there is more optimism about a possible agreement on the issues raised during the "lauriting duck" session, with the ratification of the "New START" pact.
William Galston of the Brookings Institution cautioned that fruitful cooperation on the most pressing issues of the "Ducky blade" session did not necessarily give rise to a better understanding between the two parties when the 112th Congress met in January.
Instead, he says, a period of disaggregation is likely or even inevitable.
'Once the things that need to be done are, psychology in the short term will return to one of the confrontations,' he said.
Wehner had also expressed his widely shared scepticism that civility would lead to genuine co-cooperation.
"I see most of what's going on between today and 2012 as a pre-spectacle or shadow-boxing compared to the main event of 2012," he said.
The reasons are closely related to the composition of the new Republican Majority in the House and to the tea voters who contributed to its creation.
However, Republican leaders are aware of the dangers if they go too far, they know that they must try to live up to their promises to reduce public spending and to abrogate the new health-care law.
These partisan positions are already clear in the initial reactions to the Deficit Committee's recommendations which include reforming the tax code and increasing certain taxes, raising the retirement age and reducing spending.
Co-Presidents Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles chose one of the most difficult tasks in D.Chift., in their view, imposed the deficit issue on the public agenda in a way that cannot be ignored.
But they need 14 votes in the 18-member committee to issue their plan as an official recommendation, and they have found it difficult to reach that number.
The greatest resistance remains among the elected representatives of the group, which suggests that there are on tax issues, at least today, few motives or willingness to compromise.
Galston anticipated two phases of fighting between the White House and congressional congressional Republicans.
The first comes next year in the form of partisan clashes, given that Republicans challenge Obama in terms of budgets, taxes, and health-care; and then followed by a period of greater bipartite co-cooperation.
What would be the cause of the change?
Public opinion.
There is ample evidence that, despite the partisan spirit of the recent election, a majority of Americans still want to see both parties work together.
If confrontation leads to an impasse, as in 1995, then the two parties will carefully observe the reaction of the public.
Whoever is judged to be at a loss will have to change course and find compromises.
Nevertheless, that would be an optimistic point of view.
Pleading spirit and polarization are greater today than they were in the mid-1990s, and there seems to be a greater ideological gap between Obama and congubile Republicans than it was then between President Bill Clinton and the Republicans.
At the same time, activists from both parties see 2010 as a mere prelude to a larger election in 2012.
In the light of this reality, the civility exposed this week could be appreciated by many Americans.
But it will take much greater effort on both sides to find common ground on the issues that further diivate them.
