Khmer Rouge Veterans Obliging to Confront the Past
In an ill-enhunted classroom with decrepit concrete-walls of poor quality, the headmaster of Anlong Veng High School mentioned the man who had built it, the last Khmer Rouge leader, Ta Mok.
"Everyone here loves Ta Mok."
"He was a good ruler; and he cared about his people," said 42-year-old Sreng Kor Ma.
Known as "the butcher" for his brutality, when he was at the head of the Khmer Rouge, this commander remains popular in this isolated bastion of ancient Khmer Rouge in north-western Cambodia where it built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure; and where thousands of ex-soldiers of the organization still live today.
But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge went to the government, long-standing loyalty is finally being challenged in Anlong Veng.
In April, a local forum for truth and reconciliation enabled victims to deal publicly with the people who had participated in the regime.
In June, in June, the government distributed a high-school textbook which, for the first time, teaches the history of the Khmer Rouge to the children of its ex-soldiers.
And, in July, a Cambodian court, led jointly with the United Nations, handed down its first sentence in respect of a former Khmer Rouge member, sentencing to 35 years'imprisonment the former head of the infamous Tuol Sleng torture centre, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as "Duch".
Faced with these changing circumstances, there has been angribilities among Khmer Rouge veterans in Anlong Veng, complicating reconciliation efforts and their willingness to re-integrate into Cambodian society.
"There is rejuvenations and fear among the ancient Khmer Rouge but they are powerless to do anything," said Chhang Youk, director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, an independent body that conducts research on the regime.
"Life under the Khmer Rouge was glorious for them, but the regime became a symbol of evil."
'The situation generates divisions within families.'
During Khmer Rouge reign in Cambodia from 1975 to 1979, about 1.7 million people were executed or died of starvation, disease or overburden.
When Vietnamese invaded and overthuntangled the government of Pol Pot United States in 1979, the rest of the regime and its army fled to the Cambodian border with Thailand.
They launched an errinsurgency which lasted until the last of the movements in December 1998.
While Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary were leading guerrillas from their base in the mountains of western Cambodia and in the Jungle, Ta Mok was preparing a sequel to Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s after a United Nations-sponsored United Nations-sponsored peace agreement led to the first democratic elections in the country in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began defecating the government, culminating in capitulations of Ieng Sary in 1996 and Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan in 1998.
Pol Pot died of natural causes in 1998 and Ta Mok was captured by Government forces in 1999 in the mountains near Dangrek.
He dwelt in Phnom Penh prison in 2006 pending his trial.
Without their benegotiators, most Khmer Rouge veterans have sank into abject apprehension, common to the rural regions of Cambodia; and many remain nostalgic for the movement.
Although some elite Khmer Rouge officers retained their positions as local rulers in exchange for laying down their arms, they were unskilled low-ranking farmers.
'These individuals received very little follow-up after capitulation,' said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, which organizes public forums throughout Cambodia, including in Anlong Veng.
"They are imbued with the agrarian utopian ideology of the regime."
"They've been brunted in a setting where doing business is a foreign concept."
Sor Lim, a 55-year-would, who joined the Khmer Rouge as a teenage breeze in 1974, competed in the jungle around Anlong Veng before settling in 1998 as a poor rice grower.
"Life under the Khmer Rouge was good," he said.
"Ta Mok nourishes everyone."
But now life is difficult because we have to support ourselves."
The current Khmer Rouge court also caused problems here.
At the beginning of next year, the court is expected to begin the trial of Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister.
The mandate of the tribunal is to try the top leaders and those most responsible for the crimes, but it did not say whether it continued beyond these four cases.
It would be more necessary to allay fears in Anlong Veng.
Recent media speculation turned to 64-year-would Im Chaem, a former district chief in Banteay Meanchey province during Khmer Rouge reign in the 1970s.
In 2007, it told researchers at the Cambodia Documentation Centre that it had oversimplified the construction of the Trapeang Thma dam, a project in which thrifted thousands of forced labour.
Recently, during a torrid party, Im Chaem came back from work in the field located near his wooden house on stilshade, on a dirt road outside of Anlong Veng.
As the sun threw long shades over dirted grass, Im Chaem refused to discuss its past within the Khmer Rouge.
If the court summoned it, it said, it would refuse to go there.
'Cambodia is at peace and stable,' she said.
"If there are more prosecutions, there will be war."
Prime Minister Hun Sen deflected the Khmer Rouge in 1978 has also repeatedly warned that instability would occur if the court pursued several suspects, although experts and historians contest this.
'Cambodia must dig a hole and bury the past,' he said.
Confronting the past is all that Cambodia has to do to move forward, says Youk Chhang from the documentation center.
'Reconciliation in Khmer terms is to reconnect broken pieces,' he said.
"We have to put these pieces together so that we can understand."
The center produced the first government-enhanced manual on the Khmer Rouge, "A 75-page History of Democratic Kampuchea."
He distributed it to Anlong Veng in June as a supplement to the history textbooks of the Ministry of Education, designed for higher schools, which contained less than four pages on Khmer Rouge.
As in most of Cambodia, young people in Anlong Veng know only a few details about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city's ties to the regime.
Touch Valeak, a 19-year-would student at Anlong Veng High School, stated that the new textbook helped students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted skeptically when he came back with the book at home to study.
'My family rejects many parts of the manual and the court,' he says.
"They are suspicious because they don't know exactly how many people the court will continue."
Such resistance has made the reconciliation process complex and difficult, said Sok Leang.
But the public forums, the textbook and the court are beginning to have an impact, he said.
Yet the Khmer Rouge retain a powerful wretched here.
In Dangrek's mountain heights, a weed-surllest path and strewn with plastic sacs, leads to a black soot rectangle, coated with rusted sheet-metal roof.
The body of Pol Pot was burned here on a pile of tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, a 31-year-wouldrift mobile phone saleswoman, said that she didn't know much about the man who was cremated there.
'But it has a great wizard; and a lot of people go there to pray," she said.
It was Nuom Sothea's birthday and, later that that that that day, she planned to walk to Pol Pot's last resting where she would leave a bouquet of ripe bananas in the hope of bringing luck.
Belarus Agrees to forfeit its military-grade Uranium stockpile
The government of Belarus announced on Wednesday that it will abandon its stock of highly enriched uranium, an essential component for nuclear weapons; this is part of a surprise victory of President Obama's campaign to secure nuclear materials around the world
The deal was legged in a joint communiqué issued after Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton met here with Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov on the sidelines of a security summit.
The U.S. government had been trying for years to convince Belarus to give up highly enriched uranium and seemed to have cleared the skyline last spring.
But U.S. officials have told the Washington Post that some classified operations have been carried out with Belarus in the last two months to remove 187 pounds of military-grade uranium from a research center in Belarus.
This paved the way for an agreement on Wednesday to eliminate the rest of the material by about 500 pounds, according to an official who raised the condition of an anonymity due to the sensitivity of the issue.
The agreement seems to be one of the most important outcomes of the 47-nation Nuclear Security Summit, organized at the initiative of Obama, where Belarus was excluded.
Apparently, it was profitable to obtain an invitation to the former Soviet republic following the summit in 2012.
"I'm really fascinated by the fact that they want a siege at the 2012 Nuclear Security Squad Nuclear Squadron."
'This proves that this process works,' said Ken Luongo, Chairman of the Global Partnership for Global Security, a Nuclear Security Promotion Group.
Clinton described the agreement as "a very significant, important step" carried out by Belarus.
Obama pledged in Prague in April 2009 to secure all vulnerable nuclear materials around the world over the next four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has been helping six countries get rid of all of their highly enriched uranium.
Belarus would be the seventh.
About a dozen countries did so under the administration of George W. Bush.
At the time of Obama’s Nuclear Security Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko has stated that the nation will never give up its uranium.
'We've kept highly energized uranium, hundreds of kilograms of what's mostly military-grade uranium and low-enriched uranium,' said Lukashenko.
"It's our product ... We're not going to make dirty bombs, and we're not going to sell it to anyone."
'We're just going to use it for research purposes. That's all.'
Belarus approached the United States a few months ago; however, expressing an interest in reaching an agreement.
The first implementation to remove uranium from Belarus in October was particularly complicated because the equipment had been irradiated slightly irradiated, officials said.
It was loaded on a special train that travelled more than 1,300 mile to an installation in Russia for storage and disposal, officials said.
The second operation was completed on Monday and was carried out by an aircraft, according to officials.
The U.S. government has expedged USD 13 million in its efforts, in large part to replace the research facilities in Belarus with unenhanced low-enforced uranium; unsuitable for weapons.
"Fictitious co-ordination and organisation have been implemented to build confidence," said Andrew Bieniawski, head of the Global Threat Reduction Energy Department.
According to the James Martin Non-Proliferation Study Centre estimates, Belarus this year had at least 88 pulses of military-grade uranium, as well as hundreds of kilograms of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus is seeking to eliminate its stocks by 2012, according to a joint statement.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been tense for years, mainly due to Lukashenko's harsh repression of human rights.
But Wednesday’s statement suggested a potential renewal.
"While welcoming the progress on these global security issues, the United States and Belarus recognised that increased respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remain essential for improving bilateral relations; and indispensable for the progress of the country and its citizens", she said.
