Red Bull Triumph in F1 Victory with Secret Weapon from Siemens
Software creates real-time improvements.
As soon as Red Bull entered the Formula One world in 2004, its history in this select universe of speed was marked by surprise and success.
In the first moment, no one took into account the options of a new team that had just arrived, despite the upward trajectory of the last few seasons last November, in the final phase of the league they all thrilled over Fernando Alonso's Ferrari triumph.
Without diminishing the proficiency of the champion, the German Sebastien Vette, the capacity of the Renault engine that propelled his car in Formula One, we talk about the legend of "secret weapon" that the multinational company Siemens put in the hands of Red Bull to transform it into a winning team.
It is in fact a powerful process simoving software which the German company launched on the market in 2007 with objectives very far removed from high automobile competition.
Appropriated PLM, this powerful computing instrument has been used to create assembly lines from all-wholesophifi manufacturing plants from all over the world, energy stations and high-precision instruments.
The objective is to reproduce, faithfully simulating the product or the process before constructing it in a real way, thus avoiding preventable defects and costs.
PLM facilitated the development of Canon cameras, Sukhoi planes or Nissan's mostfamous utility vehicles.
Siegfried Russwurm, Head of the Industrial Industry Division of Siemens, explains the fact that the use of this computing instrument in its Formula 1 plant enabled Red Bull to create and execute "within few hours, almost in real-time", the modifications, changes and improvements detected during racing and testing.
Russwurm explained that the teams of the year 1992 had an average of 10 tech technicians in their teams who were capable of introducing a maximum of three or four improvements on vehicles each season.
In the Red Bull team currently work 180 high-quality young professionals with powerful computing equipment analysing and projecting all sorts of modifications on the cars.
The manager of Siemens explained that the PLM software is capable of analyzing and resuscitivating all the elements of Red Bull cars except the engine, which is Renault's sole responsibility.
Materials, turbulent, aerodynamics and safety elements are modified.
Chankises can move from the virtual reality of simulator to reality in less than 24 hours, thanks to advanced high-tech laser and autoclave production systems.
As a single limitation of this magnificence of technological refinement there is the amount of 40 million dollars that each Formula One team can spend as a boundary every season to facilitate competition.
Red Bull installed the high-tech plant to create and produce its carbodies in a locality 50 kilometers from London.
These are two buildings where visits, especially for journalists, are restricted.
Everything that's going on out there is considered "top secret."
There are 580 staff on this assignment, including two drivers, champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
Red Bull Formula One hero, arguably, is the recent champion Sebastian Vetell.
Among the immense technological resources that the energy beverage brand has put at the service of its participation in the circuits, there are other hidden protagonists whose activity is priceless and notoriety.
Factory Chief of Technology Steve Nevey said that five years ago it could be said that progress in racing cars brought 50% of the victories and drivers were responsible for the rest.
Today these percentages have changed, and the technology represents 85% of success, while pilots bring the 15% of added value.
Nevey confesses that the Vettel or Webber will never be replaced by computers, because "they are unforgivable gladiators".
HP will install in two years 20 clean stores with local distributors
Hewlett-Packard (HP) has inaugurated its first exclusive store in Barcelona in collaboration with partner Pont Reyes.
It will be the first in the network of between 80 and 100 square metres between 80 and 100 square metres that the multinational intends to install in Spain over the next two years.
The company will count this initiative with the 16 areas already present in multi-bratory centres such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
The Vice-President of HP Spain, Helena Herrero, gave as an example the renovated store located on the Gran Via of Barcelona, owned by Pont Reyes, with which she has been collaborating for 30 years.
The North American bundle prepared a plan to establish 200 branded outlets in Europe in 2013, a strategy that includes all 20 Spanish companies.
The first opened were those from Bucharest (Romania) and Belgrade (Serbia) over the past week, the one in Barcelona being the third.
Herrero didn't want to unvelocate the next few stores, even though she confessed that Madrid might be the next destination.
The director of HM Personal Systems assured that the philosophy for the location of shops will always be the same, with the support of a local distributor with experience and knowledge of the sector.
The North American group advises and manages the design of the exclusive furniture of shops (with products that can be affected in an interactive way), which in Europe will hold the same image.
In addition, HP covers the training of institutional staff.
Helena Herrero denied that it is a franchise system and pointed out that the exclusive network of shops claims to "improve the service offered to customers and SMEs in procurement, assistance, post-sales and even packaging".
The shop in Barcelona includes a space with a world-famous pilot programme that solves the possible problems of HP's computer equipment under warranty.
An on-the-shelf service connects with a technical centre located in Maastricht (Holland).
"Thus, in 10 minutes we can solve problems that can often take even two days."
'This system is designed specifically for MPEs and users, because big companies have already contracted technical support services,' noted Helena Herrero yesterday.
Sabadell bets 'flight' a million customers to its rivals in three years
More optimitary than other executives, Banco Sabadell executive Jaime Guardiola believed that 2011 would be a tough year for the banking sector, but initially rejected a new liquidity crisis and predicted the end of the passive war.
Despite this, it anticipates a collapse of interest margins, which will be used to partially offset with commission increments and credit prices.
Sabadell is expected to pick up one million individual customers in three-year-old jobs.
Sabadell has found a way forward for the next three years and thus be able to survive in a very complicated economic environment.
In 2011, the bank will start a new master plan with a three-year potential.
The objective, apart from surviving without the need to use FROB's public subsidies, is to gain a market rating by capturing one million new individual customers - 700 000 if we deduce those who can leave the bank - its great task to accomplish, because until now this bank had spoiled more SMEs and companies than the particular customer.
This year it caught 25,000 individual customers and 50,000 companies.
Its retail market rating is 3.5 per cent, while in companies it exceeds 10 per cent.
Another basis of the 2011–2013 plan is the bet on productivity, or what is the same, of austerity, with the reduction of costs as an objective, the provocations of which will be explained by the bank when it presents the results of the exercise.
Few banks now dail dare to set targets and make them public, because the financial crisis has disturbed all the plans of the global banking sector.
Sabadell, in fact, will finalise its Optima master plan this year, established four years ago, even though initially the plan was for three years, but the market situation determined its extension for one more fiscal year.
Customers leasing savings banks will be its main objective.
During a Breakfast organised by APIIE, Guardiola stressed that 2011 will be a "difficult and transcendental" year.
In fact, the latter believes that the exit from the crisis in Spain "wouln't be slow".
Moreover, in two or three years' time it will be "very difficult to create jobs", which is fundamental to the survival of the banking business, he insisted.
And even though he thinks that passive warfare – which penalizes margins – is shrunk, the price of risk premonaes that banks and coffers are paying for its issues to obtain liquidity are already moving at the price of credit to compensate for falling margins.
'Fintermediacy and financing aspect is structural and will be a factor of pressure on credit.'
"We didn't clamp the faucet, but the differentials are taller," said Guardiola.
Banesto has already instructed its network to limit credit in the long term and raise the price of differentials - see CincoDas on 22 and 27 November.
He also confessed that the current trend of the banking sector "is to increase commissions", but "there is a competitive framework".
Sabadell’s master plan is based on organic growth even if, as always, the group does not eliminate the possibility of carrying out corporate operations.
It is not in vain that in Spain 'greable dimensions will be solicited.
'While everything will change next year,' he said.
He claimed that capital requirements "great increased enormously" and some Spanish financial entity could enter red numbers.
And he mentioned that FROB could replace the market in capving funds in the case of new recapitalisations.
After assuring that Sabadell can survive the temporal closure of the markets, it has diminished the importance of the fact that some banks are already predicting as a new liquidity crisis.
It points out that it is not possible, but in contrast to what happened in June, short-term funding can be obtained through rest, which alleviates the crisis.
ECSC rejects the possibility of a second wave of melting funds
The Director-in-Chief of ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, rejected yesterday the possibility of a new wave of mergers of Savings Banks and once again insisted on the speed with which the restructuring of the sector is being carried out.
When the Spanish Savings Convenience Contest Process began, there were a number of entities that ensured that they were not going to participate in this first phase and reserved for the second phase.
However, the Director-General of the Espagnole Confederation of Caisses d'Épargnoes (CECA), José Antonio Olavarrieta, yesterday rejected the possibility of producing this new wave of operations.
'I don't think that most of the meshes have been carried out, we can't say that the incorporation of Cajasol into the Banca Cavia group is a second phase, these are adjustments.'
'Most has been fully achieved; it is possible that instead of 17 we will go to 13 or 14, but all the major processes have already been carried out under the supervision of the Bank of Spain, which collaborated a great deal in this operation as well", stressed the leader.
In the declarations during a breakfast of the Association of Aragon Leaders and Executives (ADEA), Olavarrieta explained that the process of concentration is carried out 'at the highest possible speed and we need to be very satisfied with the way it has been achieved within the deadlines set in the past, not now, in the business plans with the Bank of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.
'The shift of 45 bundles with an averaging 28,500 million assets to 17 with 75,500 million is an unparalleled lawsuit in history, by reducing entity numbers by 62% so that they are much larger to conform to Basel,' he stressed.
Olavarrieta acknowledged that "there is a number of unpleasant opinions about the slowness of the process" which demonstrate 'lack of serosity and meaning', because 'what the Bank of Spain is reminding us that the deadline must be respected before the end of the year.
The Director-in-Chief of the ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, yesterday assured that Spain has the most rigorous financial system in all Europe and "probably" in the world, and he attributed to the people who cast doubt on it a "lack of acquaintance".
The leader mentioned that given the current economic situation, "we can remain very quiet" with the situation of financial entities and savings co-princiferations, a system that has started a "very important" merger process and 'go at the greatest possible speed.
He also pointed out that the Spanish banking sector did not depart from the stress tests carried out by ECB in July.
zapatero and the German red line
Berlin has chosen to remain in the euro, but it has established the conditions for the whole of the Union.
Tensibilities in the markets led to a doble government response.
On the one-to-one basis, José Luis Rodriguez has announced new adjustment methods, which vary from tax reductions for SMEs, regulation of private employment agencies and the abolition of EUR 426 of assistance to the unemployed without a professional contribution to the privatisation of 49% of AENA, the management of Barajas and El Prat airports, and the 30% State Lotteries.
On the other hand, Vice-President Elena Salgado, with the support of former President Felipe Gonzalez, unites with Chancellor Angela Merkel criticizing Chancellor Angela Merkel’s responsibility for postponing the current crisis.
How should these movements be interpreted?
It is worth mentioning that Merkel’s position is not a mistake in her communicative policy.
On the contrary, this is another restriction of the difficult process of redefining the euro.
And here Germany has a radically new vision of what its future should be, shocking that of maintaining the old traditions of most of its partners.
Faced with European resistance, in Germany we are beginning to speculate on the three possible exits of the crisis.
The first, a strong euro, integrated by itself, Austria and Holland, among other countries, and the south would abandon the monetary zone.
The second, the recovery of the Mark, even though it now sounds like science fiction, with a very favorable current of opinion, nearly 50% of the population.
The third, a new conception of the euro, in line with the German proposals for greater macroeconomic control, budgetary rigour, the fines to the countries that infringe them, the possible suspensions of payments from one of them and the extension to the private sector of the costs of potential adjustments.
The German position is clear, for a long time since it was defined, so now there is no room for an apology.
First of all, in 2009, when both an IMF and the European Commission stimulated deficits, the German government invented a constitutional reform to prevent them from 2016.
This was the first signal of a divergence between what Germany thought it should be done (fiscal policies and stability of public finances) and what its European partners, including France, were practicing.
The second shot of the Central European country was when Merkel struggled to prolong the decision to salvage Greece.
From that moment it was the subject of ferocious criticization, but the truth is that the political consensus in Germany and public opinion and published it were against the rescue of Greece.
Finally, towards the end of October, Merkel once again expresses a clear position, by calling for the reform of the Union’s treaties in order to make possible the rescue of a country and, at the same time, force private lenders to bear a share of the cost.
This position has been interpreted as anti-European because it precipitated the Irish crisis and the rest.
But its proposal, although a little watered-down, was approved and, over the next few months, we should be witnessing the reform process of the Union, which will make these measures possible from 2013.
Germany has drawn a red line which it does not intend to come across.
And it tells us all that, even if now we have to take measures with which we may not agree too much, from that date the old functioning of the euro would have ended.
2013 will mean the emergence of a new monetary zone, in which countries that violate the rules will be severely penalised, they may be forced to withdraw from payments and, in such a case, the private sector will also bear a party of costs.
Germany tells us that we need to follow a strong path of growth with high rates of savings and investment, moderation in consumption, improvements in productivity and competitiveness and positive external balances.
An alternative based based on debt and construction, with huge gaps in the balance-of-payments, such as the one that presided over Spanish growth in the years of the boom, will simply not be tolerated.
The bet is clear.
And it wouldn't be wrong not to hear it.
It is not just a question of renewing the government deficit by 3% in 2013.
What we are being asked is much tougher.
And what it absolutely must not do in these circumstances is to deceive ourselves.
I hope the current turbulent turbulent cause for greater tranquillity.
And let us grasp the opportunity we have been given, until the spring of 2013.
It also seems that the zapatero president has also heard from this form and its twofold statement (the reforms promised after the meeting of the past Saturday with big business with big business and yesterday’s announcement of new measures) suggests that, despite Merkel’s critics, we have paved the way to the the thesis to be put to order at home.
But the red line that Germany drew is there, and they don't seem to displace it.
Among the possibilities that we are contemplating, it is not possible to rule out the possibility of abandoning an overburdened monetary zone of unserrogating countries.
And in that case, Germany will always be able to tell us that it warned us.
Sport and fraud of the law
Ruses similar to that of Alonso and Ramos against Ajax demonstrate ingenuity, but not the desire to escape punishment.
Expulsion breeze in principle by two players from Real Madrid (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) during the December 23 game against Ajax in the Champions leaked a great deal of ink on the anti-sportive nature of such behaviour and whether both players should be sanctioned by UEFA.
As is well known, a few minutes before finishing the match (which their team was already clearly winning), the two players deliberately lost the time when they made mistakes to determine the refereee to show them the second yellow card.
Thus, given that Real Madrid will end up being leader of his group, the two players will perform the regulatory sanction during the next game of the play-off against Auxerre and will be able to pass without boxes in the next phase.
Eventually, UEFA Discipline Committee punished team coach José Mourinho with two matches and various economic sums for the players involved.
Provoking a yellow card in circumstances such as this or the like is not a new practice, if not rather frequent in the world of soccer.
It is so widespread that it is part of ethos, unwriting rules, but followed, of this sport.
Part of the debate that has arisen in this case is due to the protagonist of a team from the entity of Real Madrid (which in its anthema displays nobility and honour and which on past occasions has fined its players for similar acts) and the orchestrated dramatization, it seems, by Mourinho, who adds an additional episode to his technician.
In any any case, the debate asks whether this type of conduct expressly prohibited by the regulation contradicts the principles of sport, i.e. whether with these actions a fraud of a law is committed; because, in fact, they are respectful actions with the sporting regulations, but seem to avoid its meaning.
The question is to examine the type of harm they cause in the sporting spirit.
One of the goods that understands the sporting spirit is the physical integrity of the opposing players, an aspect that with these actions did not put into jeopopardising, i.e. there has been no violent game.
Another of these items to which reference is made with the correct game is to prevent the team of intracting players from taking unfair advantage of the team to the contrary, which is not the case either.
However, the paradoxical effect of the actions of the Real Madrid players is that it does not live up to what is normally characterised as cheating on law fraud, because its violation of the rules has not been carried out in a hiding place in an attempt to avoid the penalty, but their action has precisely tried to be as obvious as possible in order to receive the judicator.
They were hurry and willingly accepted the punishment.
Another question is the kind of advantage that offenders obtain the intentional errors examined.
Here it seems necessary to note that one does not put the rival at a disadvantage in a situation of disadvantage and one does not obtain an advantage in the same match either, because of course the result of the action presupposes leaving their own team with fewer players.
And this isn't a useful strategy for the next game either, because the club couldn't rely on these players.
Of course, the advantage lies in the fact that the players will begin the next phase of the Champions League without boxes that could threaten their participation in a future game.
But this is an advantage in the long term, with a degree of indeterminacy, because the team could be eliminated all of a sudden and apart from that, with this action, the players complete a penalty match and move to the second cycle of cardboard, in which the suspension due to the build-up of the cartons occurs with one less than the first cycle.
So climbing the ladder isn't as clear that this is an advantage.
In the end, these actions do not run counter to the spirit of sport, from which strategic decision-making nevertheless includes the taking of strategic decisions that promote the maximum benefit of what the rules permit.
In our case, the gamblers did not activate in order to avoid the application of the sanction (this would indeed be a clear case of fraud of the law), otherwise they chose instead the moment in which they submitted to it.
They made a strategic decision without foiling the penalty or escluding it.
Even though the boundary between genius and fraud of the law is diffused, on this occasion I think there was a clear genius genius.
The polemic, it seems to me, is apparent from two factors: because there were two self-expulations and because of the manner of transmitting the director to provoking them.
The harm caused to the principles of sport by these actions is not as ethical as aesthetic.
