Pakistan's nuclear program causes great concern in the United States
Islamabad produces atomic weapons "familder than any other country".
Wikileak filtrations confirm the connection between the Taliban and Pakistan.
The content of the correspondence between the American Embassy in Pakistan and the United States State Department filtered through the Wikileaks portal confirms the enormous worries caused in D.C. in D.C., Pakistan’s nuclear program.
Apart from enlightening and tasty details on the content of the conversations and thoughts of North American diplomatic representatives accredited in Islamabad, the documents reveal the fact that cooperation between the two countries in the field of atomic security, instead of an increase, is "receivable" and that the unstable Asian country is producing nuclear weapons "at a faster rate than any other country in the world".
The peculiarity of the Pakistani nuclear programme - the fact that the weapons are not assembled, but they are packed with components to make their destruction difficult in the event of an attack - is one of the main reasons for worries in D.D.
For example, the Ambassador to Pakistan, Anne W. Patterson, listed in a report prior to visiting former National Security Councillor, James Jones: “The proximity of certain nuclear installations to the territory under Taliban attack, the dispersal of nuclear materials whose current noise and the vulnerability of the transport of atomic materials”.
The large number of people involved in nuclear arsenals and missile programmes is envisaged, not not only by the United States, but also by the United States, as proliferating and a reason for maintaining custody.
"Shiven had to hire staff with strict religious beliefs to protect nuclear facilities; given the decline in educational and cultural levels, extremist organizations have more opportunities to recruit people who work in nuclear or missile programs", said expert Yuri Korolev of the Russian Ministry of Externals during a meeting with his American counterparts.
The new documentation legged through Julian Assange's bundle is the increasingly reduced cooperation between the United States and Pakistan in the field of nuclear proliferation and the high level of production of nuclear weapons in Pakistan.
A dispatch from the Embassy in May, which qualifies the communication with Pakistan as 'furgitant', argues: 'Transparency is often non-existent; offers of assistance remain unanswered or eliminated'.
In another message, Peter Lavoy, National Intelligence for Southern Asia official, notes that “despite the economic catastrophe, Pakistan produces nuclear weapons at a faster rate than any other country in the world”.
Ambassador Patterson also calls in Washington, D.C., during a visit by the Army leader, General Ashfaq Kayani, to convey "very clearly" the official that "the Pakistani Army must end with tacit support for terrorist groups".
