Interpol issues a red alert to track movements and locate in Assange.
All aspects related to Wikileaks and its found founder, Julian Assange, forms a labyrinth in which it is difficult to obtain certainty about the facts.
Yesterday Interpol issued a "Red Alert" to 'locate and track Assange's movements.
However, this is not a traditional arrest order.
In addition, it is not new, otherwise it dates from 20 November.
And this one has nothing to do with the latest filtration of more than a quarter of United States diplomatic documents, but with the accusations of sexual abuse feign in Sweden.
In addition, Wikileaks creator, Mark Stephens, claimed that "the police know where and how to contact Asssange".
The 20 the international police force based in Lyon (France) dictated the order at the request of Sweden, but it did not make it public until yesterday, after having received the permission of that country's authorities.
For example, the 188 Interpol countries were instructed to identify or locate Assange "to obtain his provisional arrest and extradition".
Even if Interpol cannot order the arrest of an individual affected by a Red Alert, many Member States, according to the organization's explanations, consider the Red Notice call a free route for the arrest -- which the national police force must realize - especially if they have a bilateral extradition agreement, as is the case in Sweden and England, where Assange is believed.
The movement outraged Assange lawyers, who yesterday issued a letter denunciating that the 39-year-old founder of Wikileaks had been willing to respond by video-conferencing to questions from the Swedish authorities.
They would also point out that Assange solicited and obtained a permit to leave the prosecutor's case from Sweden, which is compared to Stalin's famous Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria.
'This seems to be a chase, not legal persecution,' the missive says.
While the world is searching for him - and in Australia his mother defends the innocence of his son -- Assange continues to be in an unmarked place, but he doesn't keep quiet.
On the Monday he gave an undetermined place an interview with the director of the magazine Time through Skype, a conversation in which he requested the resignation of United States Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.
"While it can be shown that it has been responsible for giving orders to American graduates that they spy on the United Nations by infringing the international treaties that the United States has signed, it should resign," he said.
His opinion was insulted yesterday by White House Press Secretary Robert Gibbs, who described the demand for a rebirth of the rebirth.
'I can't be at all sure why the opinion of a guy with a web page should be of interest to us,' he told CNN.
Obama himself has instructed a senior anti-terror leader, Rusell Travers, to prevent further leaking of documents.
President President Barack Obama’s spokeswoman’s attempt to reduce Assange contrasts with the opinions that conservative politicians and commentators are proclaiming these days.
Peter King, the congresswoman who from January will preside over the National Security Committee in the Lower House has asked the State Department to study the possibility of including Wikileaks in the list of terrorist organizations "for confiscating its funds and prosecuting to anyone who provides them with contributions or assistance".
Even further went to Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian President Stephen Harper, who had to reneglect his statements after saying that Assange should "be assassinated".
"Obama should turn it into a lens lens and wear an unmanned airplane or something similar."
One person who didn't seek an apology was Mike Huckabee, Baptist Minister, ex-Governor of Arkansas, and possible presidential candidate.
"He who is in the Government that has lewisted the information is guilty of treason, and all that not an exerclutant would be a punishment too gentle," he said for Fox.
Puigcercs seeks peace in ERC with winks to Carod followers
The republican leader will allow the party a secret vote on its continuity.
Huguet proposed a more plural executive and the end of the assembly system.
Joan Puigcerces seems to have found a recipe for resolving the umpteenth internal revolt inside ERC, no north due to the loss of 11 MPs and half of the votes.
The chairman of the party is aware of the fact that his threat of rebirth is not sufficient for the followers of former leader Josep Lluas Carod-Rovira, but at the same time he knows that the influence of this sector is now as divulged as its liar, and that it absolutely cannot give it any headaches that he had with other extinated currents.
This is why Esquerra's leader will try to win his new reviews with nods that allow him to blind up to the municipal elections.
As a first step, Puigcers announced yesterday that more than 200 ERC national councillors will be able to comment on its continuity in secrecy.
By means of ballot papers, not by raising one's hand.
If carodists want to convey a certain penalizing vote to the president, they will be able to do so without any feorgive retalience.
In fact, the leader wants to avoid an image of divisiveness and that of subjugating.
In a number of interviews, he stated that it would not consider itself ratified with half plus one of the votes, but it added, without mentioning the percentages, that an "Bulgarian" result would not be good either.
Those close to Carod demand a little more from the president than to make his position available for militancy, a gesture that Puigcers doesn't value when Puigcers controls the maximum body between the congresses.
But because they don’t have a sufficient majority to impose resignations, they will try to agree with the executive a road map until the month of May.
To that end, they hoped to participate in the preparation of the strategic reshuffling document that the management would submit to the vote of the national council on 18.
Its intention was to support Puigcercas in exchange for its promise to schedule the congress for the fall of 2011, which the leaders saw as a sure fact.
The ERC leader confessed that "polyphony" affects stability and that its goal is to make the organization once and for all capable of washing its laundry with the family.
In this line, the article that current adviser Josep Huguet signed in his blog is ympamitic.
Looked up by Puigcers' ideologue, the holder of Innovaci thought that the fact that the leader would be ratified "in exchange for various purposes" was as if it had happened.
And it has listed five of them, of which it highlights two.
The first, “recompose” the executive to represent at least 90% of the militancy, i.e. include new figures from Carod's practicing sector.
The latter may be the character of great news.
Huguet proposes that ERC liquid once and for all the assembly as an operating mechanism.
Thus, it defends a reshaping of the statutes that will make it possible to organize the congresses through delegates elected by vote.
Finishing with the assembly model, an exception in the Spanish party system, was an obsession of the Puigcerus team, which has already struggled in this direction in the last two congresses.
In fact, the convulse conclave of 2008 instructed the management to revise the assembly model in a specific congress for the re-shaping of the statutes.
This meeting remains uncompleted because the internal tide of recent years did not recommend addressing this issue.
Now, without critic sectors on the lookout, the time could be a good time to pick it up and close it forever.
