China Maintains Greenhouse Gas Reduction Targets
The country's representative in COP16 assured that his country had made enormous efforts to reduce the trace of coal and had detailed that it maintained its offer of reducing its greenhouse gas emissions between 40 and 45 per cent until 2020.
China maintains its offer to reduce greenhouse gas emissions between 40 and 45 per per cent before 2020, assured Su Wei, a representative of that country at COP16.
In its first public appearance in the 16th Review Conference of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP16), Su Wei assured that his country had made enormous efforts to reduce the trail of charcoal and reconvert its industry.
In fact, he said, over the past four-year-would his country has inverted USD 245 million in efforts to reduce the emissions of greenhouse gases.
Nevertheless, it has made it clear that China will not alter its objectives, but it will continue to work together with the international community in this area.
He mentioned that China's strategy on climate change is divided into four areas: dialogue; capacity-building; institution-building and support for pilot programmes.
Progress has been made in all these areas, including the approval last year of a new Energy Law, which recognises the objectives addressed for the reduction of greenhouse gases, he explained.
On the other hand, he stressed that in China, the federal authorities had worked in a coordinated manner with local governments to achieve the goals of energy savings, efficient energy use and emission reduction.
In addition, it has said, there are various multi-annual plans underway or completed, both financed by the Chinese government and by the governments of other countries.
“For example,” he said, “Equittable Energy Utilization, which has been implemented over the past five years and has received investments from the United Nations), the government and the private initiative.
There is also a provincial project on climate change, another for the change of incandes incandescent lamps by gentlurred lamps and another mitigation and adaptation, he detailed.
At the same table, Martin Krol, scientific adviser to the country’s government on climate change, assured that China’s efforts have been greater.
For example, he said, the per capita emission of greenhouse gases in China was three tons per person, while in the United States it was 100 tons; China was responsible for the 9% of the historical contribution to the contamination.
Similarly, it has made unprecedented efforts in the age of energy, how to increase an increase of 20% energy efficiency over the last five years and plans to do a similar one around 2020, he said.
