The European Union changes the rules for obtaining a visa
Individuals must go to the Visa Applicant Service Centres prior to their Interview in the Consular Section; the waiting time costs will be less reduced.
As from 10 January 2011, the visa processing procedure at the United States Embassy and Consular Consularies will change.
Under the new procedure, the vast majority of requisitioners will have to report to the Visa Applicant Service Centres (ASC) prior to their Interview in the Consular Section.
CSA staff will take the biometric information of the applicant, which will be reviewed by the Consular Section prior to the Interview.
The new procedure will have certain advantages for aspirants, including the following:
The total cost of processing the visa will be lower, it will no longer be necessary to make a payment to obtain an appointment, an alternative payment for the processing of the visa and another for the mail service to deliver the document.
The aspirant will make a one-time payment that will cover all three services.
The current fare will remain the same: the equivalent of USD 140 for a Tourist Visa, USD 150 in the case of application visas (includes visas for temporary workers) and USD 390 for merchant and investor visas.
Individuals who wish to renew their visas should not attend an Interview with the Consular Representative, they will only report to the ASC closest where they will present the documents and take their fingerprints.
Because biometric information will be taken from the CSA, applicants who want a second appointment to be interviewed in the Consular Section will remain less time than before.
In the same way, visa-consilants to the Consulates of Ciudad Juarez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer pay the extra-dollar rate of USD 26.
For further information on the new procedure for applying for a visa, please visit http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201-Visas-FAQs.
