The Liberals claim that the bill as proposed, which made it possible to detain for up to a one-year person from a ship identified as transporting illegal immigrants would violate the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.
The head of Bloc Québécois, Gilles Duceppe, has also indicated that he cannot support the bill because he is attacking the wrong target.
'We are opposed and it seems that all the opposition parties are against, so we will see what the government will do,' said Mr Duceppe.
The NDP's immigration critic, Olivia Chow, has dismissed the cripples that his party is not firm enough about illegal immigrants.
'New Democrats are in favour of taking action against smugglers, and that's what we're doing through another bill, the C-35", said Ms Chow.
'We don't support the Conservative bill because it attacks refugees and imprisons children for at least a year,' explained Ms Chow.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney has accused the opposing parties of playing politics and ignoring the will of Canadians.
"Canadians overwhelmingly tell MPs that they don't tolerate this kind of illegal migration, these criminal operations that target Canada and treat the country like a doormat."
"They expect Canada to take firm action," said Minister Kenney.
The Government had not announced a vote of confidence on its draft law, which could have led the country to elections in the event of its rejection.
However, a Conservative told QMI Agency that the government was going to call for a vote so that every Member of Parliament who opposes then explains to their constituents.
The new unit is not universally accepted
Despite the announcement of the possible creation of a permanent unit to combat corruption and collusion by Jean Charest, many observers continue to request the creation of a public commission of inquiry on the subject.
