Quebec would also ask Ottawa to amend the Criminal Code in order to be better suited to this type of crime.
Such a request would come from the prosecutor of Operation Marteau, who informed Justice Minister Jean-Marc Fournier that case law is not explicit in such investigations.
The Federal Minister of Public Security, Vic Toews, has confirmed that his Justice colleague, Rob Nicholson, is currently considering certain amendments related to this issue.
In Operation Printemps 2001, which affected biker groups, even if the Criminal Code was unsuitable, 131 persons were arrested.
Eventually enacted the Gangsterism Act.
Rivers under high surveillance
Several watercourses remain under high surveillance due to heavy precipitation in several parts of the province since Tuesday.
A landslifting occurred on Wednesday afternoon in Shawinigan in Mauricie.
Two houses were pre-emptively vacated.
In the evening, another swirling reportedly occurred in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, in Lanaudière.
A road sagged.
From Tuesday to Wednesday evening, heavy precipitation fell in several regions, i.e. 40 to 70 millimetres of rain.
Between 20 and 40 millimetres of additional rainfall were expected by Thursday evening, depending on the region.
Sectors to the north of the river and in the Ottawa Valley were the most affected.
They could accommodate up to 100 millimetres of rain in total.
In view of the addition of this precipitation to smelting already accumulated snow in some areas, there is a fear of an overwhelming of several watercourses.
Flood alert warnings were issued on Wednesday evening by Quebec Civil Security for the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in Lanaudière.
Rivours du Nord, at Saint-Jérôme, in the Laurentians, and du Chêne in Lotbinière were the subject of idle vigils and remained under surveillance.
In the Lanaudière region, heavy precipitation led to the overwhelming of certain lakes in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare and Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, a temporary bridge was washed away due to the heavy rainfall.
Ten residencys had to be evacuated as a preventive measure.
In the Outaouais, the overwhelming of certain lakes has led to the closure of several paths, particularly in the Municipality of Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
Civil Security remains on alert and monitors the situation closely, Security Adviser Annik Bouchard said.
The Quebec Flood Forecasting Centre on Wednesday issued strong flood warnings for several streams, including Maskinongé, L'Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan et du Loup.
Partially blamed
Quebec radio host Stéphane Dupont suffered a partial set-back before the Canadian Broadcast Standards Council (CCNR) for controversial remarks about social assistance and Haitians as well as his coarse on-line language.
The CCNR considers that Mr. Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team had the right to express their opinions, but that certain statements contained “inaccurate information on social assistance, unreceivable negative representations of Haitians and coarse language that does not be suitable for programmes broadcast during the day”.
The Council also concluded that a discussion on suicide, including the broadcasting of the names of two persons who had committed suicide, did not violate the broadcasting codes.
The CCNR had received a number of complaints concerning comments made during programmes broadcast between 2008 and 2010.
The first decision was handed down following a complaint by the Quebec Common Front of Social Assisted Persons in Quebec.
The group complained that the amounts mentioned in the issue were inaccurate.
Moderizers should provide sound rather than misleading information.
With regard to suicide, the animator Stéphane Dupont had ruled that suicide was a coward.
The majority of the members of the Committee had concluded that the conveyed message was that suicide should not be seen as a solution to the problems of life.
"The majority of the Committee considers that the episodes in question of 'Dupont at midday" have addressed a sensitive issue in an equitable and appropriate manner."
No reproach was therefore held against it.
The most recent dispute concerned statements that followed the earthquake in Haiti.
Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort then engaged in a media situation which elicited a great deal of reactions.
CHOI's animator stated that he didn't make any donations to help Haitians because we couldn't know for sure that the people in need would really receive the money.
The Committee believes that these comments were not abusive, but that their cumulative effect constitutes an unduly negative representation of Haitians.
"I've already purged my three-day suspend on that way."
'My file has been blank for a year and a half years,' said the moderator.
In all decisions, the Committee denounces the dissemination of coarse language.
In each case, the broadcaster CHOI-FM collaborated "beyovalued its obligation".
The consequences are minimal for the station, which, inter alia, will have to announce these decisions once during prime-hearing hours within three days.
