EUR 450 million in contracts without competitive bidding
Hydro-Québec has out-of-voluntarily awarded 450-million-dollar contracts to engineering firms for the Eastmain-1-Sarcelle-Rupert hydroelectric project.
At the heart of this avalanche of more than 100 contracts is a consortium of BPR, Dessau, SM Group and Axor that obtained the most important, that of the management of the project, for a sum of USD 295 million without competitive bidding.
This consortium called Energie gérance was set up in 2004 concurrently with Hydro-Québec's choice to entrust to the private sector its responsibility to manage this important 5-billion-dollar hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005, the Managing Energie consortium obtained the lucrative contract without a public tender.
For the Party of Quebec, resorting to company consortia poses a serious ethical problem which risks eliminating competition and causing a rise in prices.
'This is the new way of colluding and shooting down competition.'
'In other words people, instead of doing this secretly, are now doing it in an open-ended way,' pehetic MEP Sylvain Simard said yesterday at the press conference.
Earlier in the morning, Mr. Simard raised the issue in the National Assembly because he said he did find it difficult to see how the interest of the public could be served in this way.
The Minister of Natural Resources, Nathalie Normandeau, respondented in the afternoon, vehemently denying that there had been collusion on this contract.
'I'm starting to have my journey,' she said, arguing that the PQ had been 'unhonoured' and 'familed by rigor'.
She said that "would be the slightest thing" that the PQ apologises.
Continuity formula is a common practice around the world.
Usually this is a time-bound alliance that allows two or more companies to merge their respective expertise in order to respond to a call for tenders for a complex project.
The East-main project has a high level of complexity, given the breadth of work.
The garnishment is ongoing and is scheduled until 2012.
However, the contract was awarded without competition action; there has been negotiations.
Therefore, the consortium formula is no longer an exception in Quebec, even for relatively simple projects.
As revealed last week Le Devoir, the consortium formula in the consulting engineering sector is being investigated by the Government.
A quick overflight of Quebec Business Register counts more than 200 consortia, some of which seem permanent.
Dessau is working in partnership with most of its main competitors, including CIMA, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin and other Roche and Tecsult.
Each combination, and there are dozens, leads back to one and the same legal person, namely Dessau.
With regard to the Energie  managed consortium, Minister Normandeau pointed out that "Jame James's bay territory responds to particular workforce dynamics and that Hydro-Québec has responded in a particular way since the 1970s".
Eastmain's boss, Réal Laporte of the James  Bay Energy Society (SEBJ), gave an explanation that raised the eyebrows that the project's management contract had been over-aggregated on an agreement-by-confertility.
"While you're on call for tenders and there are a few bidders, your prices are going to be higher."
"you have two choices: whether you take the people who are in this market and you're saying: we're going to negotiate a satisfactory deal and at a good price."
'But the idea is that it's not the price that's important, it's people's competence,' said Mr Laporte.
This contract awarded to Energie gérance is part of a long list of contracts of more than EUR 50 000 obtained by the Parti Québécois.
The contracts were awarded by Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for Eastmain-1-Sarcelle-Rupert.
After verification, Le Devoir consisted of 109 consulting engineering contracts awarded between 2002 and March 2010 to firms or their subsidiaries, such as soil laboratories.
SNC-Lavalin reissued 22 contracts, Génivar obtained 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, not to mention its participation in Energie gégressor.
All contracts have been negotiated.
They total EUR 449,783,344, including the Contract for Energie Manrance of EUR 295,131,564.
Sylvain Simard suggested that the four companies that make up the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) are "recognized in the Quebec Liberal Party".
Minister Normandeau dropped that the PQ is also linked to those firms that “finance it,” before pointing out that it was rather its leaders.
