A Global Plan to Renovate Lighting
The United United Nations yesterday in Cancun proposed a plan to renovate domestic, commercial and industrial lighting in 100 countries in order to achieve a reduction of greenhouse gases (GHGs), almost one of the additional reduction needed to stabilize the global climate by 2020.
This ambitious plan builds in the light of the major concrete projects that the United Nations hopes to adopt in Cancun to compensate for the lack of binding reduction commitments by the industrialized countries.
According to the plan tabled yesterday, if incandescent light bulbs still monopolising 50-70% of the market were replaced by compact fluorescent bullabs or, better still, light-emitting bullabs (LEDs), the bulk of the 1.8 billion tons (Gt) of GHG emitted over the planet to illuminate humans would be avoided.
Illumination is responsible for 8% of global emissions.
This is the equivalent of 70 per 70 per cent of emissions from the global vehicle fleet.
In Copenhagen last year, major emitting countries, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, embarked on voluntary reduction plans that are expected to increase global GHG GHG emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
But it will have to subpolehiterate the next 5 Gt balance to stabilize by 2020 the increase in the climate at 2 degrees centigrade in order to prevent it from becoming out of control.
In incandescent bulbs, 95% of the energy dissatisfies in the form of heat and the rest, i.e. 5%, produces lumen.
With compact fluorescent light bulbs, energy expenditure is reduced by 75 per 50 per cent and with the new LED bulb bulbs, this energy requirement is reduced by two more times.
Compact fluorescent bullabs have an averaging from 6,000 to 10,000 hours.
New LED bulbs, such as those launched by the manufacturer Philips this week - this company participated in the United Nations study - have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consuming 80 per cent less energy than an incandescent bulb, which gives an idea of the possible economy with an alternative plan that would apply to 100 countries.
These completely new bulbs can also work with rheostats in addition to emitting very little heat.
And they don't contain any mercury.
A number of specialists have shown reservations to fluorobullad luminumbulbs because they contain mercury and need to be disposed of as hazardous waste, which is not always possible in developing countries, unless manufacturers are putting instructions in place.
But, according to the report, the main source of mercury emissions on the planet is not the bulwark bulwark, but coal-fired thermal power plants, which emit a lot of mercury to produce wasted electricity in incandescent bulwark light bulbs.
In the United States alone, 25,000 pulses emit 25,000 tons of mercury per year.
The report sets out the gains made by each country through this international programme.
For example, for example, Indonesia would save USD 1 Bill a year in Electricity Purchase Purchase Purchase Purchase and eight Million Supplies, equivalent to USD 2.5 Million Thousand Thousand.
Côte d'Ivoire awaits the announcement of the electoral results
United Nations Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has called for "without delay" the results of the presidential elections in Côte d'Ivoire to be issued after the deadline expires.
"The Secretary-General stresses the need for the Independent Electoral Commission to complete its work and to announce the provisional results without further delay, a spokeswoman for the Head of the United Nations."
"It calls upon all Ivorian parties and the institutions to show responsibility and to refrain from any initiative until the provisional results are announced."
Despite the pressure exerted by some foreign governments, the electoral commission missed the mid-night deadline without publishing the results, three days after Sunday's election.
About 40 minutes before the deadline, the President of the IEC had stated on television that the commission was "continuing to work" to announce the results.
Calls have multiplied around the world on Wednesday that the CIS finally announces the winner of the second round between incumbent Laurent Gbagbo and opponent Alassane Ouattara.
But the Gbagbo camp counts on the annulment by the Constitutional Council of “fraudulent” votes in the north, held by the former Rebellion of the Forces nouvelles (FN) since the failed coup d'état of September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, campaigning director of the Ivorian president, claimed that the intimidation of FN voters in the north of the country had deceleased the supporters of the outgoing president to vote.
The entire election could be overturned if the complaints filed by Gbagbo were upheld by the Constitutional Council.
Gbagbo's adversaries believe that the highest court in the country is non-neutral because it is led by Paul Yao N'Dré, a close political ally of Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, a man from the North who denies any link with the ex-rebels, deemed "unacceptable" the delay in the announcement of the results and asked Gbagbo to respect them, but did not go so far as to claim victory.
Its allies claim that Gbagbo is blocking the publication of the results because he knows that he has lost and that filing a complaint before the Constitutional Council, having prevented the electoral commission from disseminating the results, would be illegal.
The second round of the presidential election on Sunday was supposed to crown the process of reuniteration and reconciliation of a country that had remained split in two for years.
The United Nations informed the Ivorian political class on Wednesday that it could be held responsible for possible violence.
Laurent Gbagbo had finished in the first round on 31 October, with 38% against 32% in Alassane Ouattara, who received public support from former President Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round).
The unemployment rate remained stable in France in the 3rd trimester
The unemployment rate in France within the meaning of the International Bureau of Labour (ILO) remained stable at 9.3% in metropolitan area (9.7%, including the overseas departments), show the provisional seasonally adjusted data (CVS) published by INSEE on Thursday.
The number of unemployed in metropolitan France is 2.631 million.
Data in the second trimester were confirmed at 9.3% of the working population in metropolitan France and 9.7% with the French overseas departments.
Over a one-year period, the unemployment rate rises by 0.1 percentage point.
More broadly, 3.4 million people in Metropolitan France did not work in the third trimester but wanted to work, whether or not they were available within two weeks to work and whether or not they were looking for a job.
The situation of young people on the labour market deteriorated further, with a further increase in the unemployment rate to 24.2% in the mechanic (by 0.9 points over a three-month period, 0.5 points over a one-year period) and 25% by including the French overseas departments.
In the third trimester, 637 000 young people aged between 15 and 24 were unemployed within the meaning of the BIT.
Older people experienced a slight increase in unemployment at 6.4% in metropolitan France (0.2%) over a three-month period as well as over one-year period of 482 000 people aged 50 or over and 6.6 per six per cent with the French overseas departments.
Only those aged 25-49 enjoyed stable unemployment in the third trimester of 8.3% in metropolis (-0.1 points over a three-month period of 0.0 per cent – 0.0 point over a one-year period) and 8.8% throughout France.
INSEE no longer publishes a monthly unemployment rate since 2006, following a polemic about its method of calculation.
On the other hand, the statistical agency of the European Union, continues to do so, and on Tuesday announced a 9.8% rate for France (DOM included) in October after 9.9% in September.
