Among those who have been confronted by Mr Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, who has recently been imprisoned in a parallel case of fraudulent public procurement in Corsica.
Investigators are suspecting that his companies were used to launder money from big banditry.
The investigation is being carried out as part of a number of judicial information opened against X in April 2009 as an adjoining anonymous letter for alleged fraud in public procurement, in particular in the waste sector.
From a source close to the investigation, the investigations focus in particular on the conditions for the allocation and operation of a La Ciotat landfill, which collects waste from Aubagne as well as from certain cities of the urban community of Marseille (MPM).
Case Clotilde Reiss: The Elysée exaggerated Syrian mediation
For more than ten months from July 2009 to May 2010, Clotilde Reiss, the young French researcher who was arrested in Iran for "espionage", kept his mind in France, put diplomacy on alert, and accentuated the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
All under the watchful eye of United States diplomats.
American documents obtained by WikiLeaks and consulted by Le Monde reveal some of these crises.
They describe how the Elysée explained its strategy to the Americans, going so far as to give them advice on dealing with its own "hostage" crises in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show how the French officials sought to convince Washington, D.D. that the release of the young Frenchwoman did not compensate for the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian detained in France and wanted by the United States justice for trafficking in materials for Iranian nuclear program.
At the end of the day, the way in which the French Presidency sought to manipulate the media to accredit the idea that the Syrian president, Bashar Al-Assad, played a central role in Clotilde Reiss.
In fact, this was not the case.
A diplomatic telegram written on 12 August 2009 in Paris is titled "Iran: French comments on how to deal with hostage cases".
The Elysée adviser on strategic affairs, François Richier, accompanied by the director of the Quai d'Orsay for North Africa and the Middle East, Patrice Paoli, meet with American diplomat Kathleen Allegrone.
They explained to him the French method.
They advise the Americans to do the same to repatriate their own forcibly detained nationals in Iran.
'Make noise,' they say, 'brush constantly' in the media the words 'fundamental human rights', 'innocence', 'immediate deliberation'.
