In the past attempts at discussion through discreet channels, 'silence won't solve anything', the Iranians are 'masters in dilatory maneuvers'.
Mr Richier adds that the arrest of foreigners is 'an familiar Iranian tactic: the taking of hostages for political blackmail'.
He announces that the release of Clotilde Reiss from prison (which will take place on 16 August, the day she takes refuge at the French Embassy) "receasing the payment to Iranians of a large sum of money" (2,330,000,000 bail).
And he makes it known that the Elysée anticipates further hostage attacks, a phenomenon that 'mold is getting worse.
The Iranians could 'in the near future stop some 1,800 of some 1,800 French citizens living in Iran,' he says.
28 August 2009.
Clotilde Reiss has been in prison for 12 days, but remains stuck in Iran.
One piece of information is being disseminated: Iranian Majid Kakavand, which the United States is seeking extradition for illegal trafficking in sensitive electronic equipment to Iran, is released in Paris, on the decision of a judge.
The United States Embassy, like many observers, is questioning this troubling coincidence.
The Majid Khakavand expansion of Majid Khakavand is bad news.
Has France accepted a haggling?
The Liberation of the Iranian from that of the French?
In another interview with Kathleen Allegrone on 4 September, François Richier confirmed that this was not the case.
The Elysée, he said, not only "was completely taken by surprise by the decision of the judge", but sought to oppose it.
"There have been lively exchanges between the French Presidency and the Seal Keeper" (at the time Michèle Alliot-Marie), but 'the French Government cannot go back over the judicial decision', he observes.
Mr Richier also wants to demonstrate that Majid Kakavand remains 'brilliated' surveillance of the French intelligence services.
The Elysée adviser dials a telephone number crooks, hung up, then informed the American diplomat that the Iranian "is currently located in buildings in Paris managed by the Iranian Embassy".
While Clotilde Reiss was detained in Iran, the Elysée was sparing its efforts, through press releases and "leakites" organized in the media, suggesting that Syrian President Bashar al-Assad played an "influential" and "fluent" role as "externist".
The Americans observed this activity not without irony, and noted that the manipulation was bearing fruit, since several press articles reflected the official French version.
A telegram dated August 25, 2009 evokes the 'blitz' media of French officials.
The embassy in Paris wonders so much "gratitude" expressed to Mr Assad.
"Despite their paucious statements, French officials privately acknowledge that they have only a vague notion of what the Syrians have actually done."
The document then examines the reasons that prompted the Elysée to pour into this fiction.
The intense communication effort 'aimed at validating Sarkozy's policy of openness to Syria,' he says.
A policy launched in 2008, which "has been perceived, by many critical voices in France and abroad, as unpremature and undeserved".
By "exaggerating the Syrian role", Paris seeks to "demonstrate to Syria what praising it can attract by playing a constructive role in the region".
The aim is also to 'breather seeds of discord between Syria and Iran'.
The U.S. Embassy knows this from a good source.
On 11 August, François Richier told him: "Of course, we don't know if Syrians did anything."
"But we thanked them anyway."
'This should at at least cause confusion among the Iranians.
