No online gaming for young Koreans past midnight
The South Korean government is going to adopt a so-called "Cundrella law" that will prevent teenagers from playing online games after midnight by blocking their access, government officials said.
A bill will be submitted to Parliament in December to require online gaming companies to block access to these games after midnight for young people under the age of 16, representatives of the Ministries of Culture and Familines said.
"A lot of children play these games all night and then have difficulty studying at school and having a normal life during the day."
"We believe that this law is necessary in order to guarantee their health and their right to slept," Jo Rin, the ministerial official in charge of the bill, told AFP.
Online gaming will once again be accessible at 6 a.m.
Companies would have one year to prepare for the application of the law, he said.
The Government is also considering asking companies to limit the daily access time to these games for young people whose parents so request, he added.
It will also provide people who request free software to limit the time spent on the Internet.
South Korea is one of the most "interconnected" countries and newspapers regularly report a variety of facts related to the intensive use of the Internet.
The country is also an undisputed champion of world online gaming competitions.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals at World Cyber Games against 58 other nations.
