Euro: the ECB forced to maintain its purchases of government securities
The last meeting of the Governing Council of the European Central Bank (ECB) of the year should be an opportunity to clarify the modalities for overcoming the crisis or at least to announce the cessation of many of the non-conventional monetary-politics operations to be completed at the end of the year.
The European sovereign-debt crisis obliges, the Frankfurt issuing institution will probably not be able to allow itself so peremptory, lest it will worsen more than a tense situation today in the European bond markets.
The political cost would be all the higher.
Yesterday, on the occasion of a press conference, the French Finance Minister, Christine Lagarde, greeted the active role played by the ECB in favour of resolving the crisis of states' debts in Europe.
She didn’t want to say whether it believed that the central bank needed to do more, e.g. by increasing its sovereign-confidence-purchase program.
"I reserve my reflections for my European partners (...)".
The previous day, at his hearing by the European Parliament's Committee on Economic and Monetary and Monetary Affairs, the President of the ECB, Jean--Claude Trichet, showed no greater dissipation as to his intentions, merely pointing out that the programme for the purchase of State securities on the secondary market "continuing" and that 'we will see what we will decide' at this Thursday's meeting.
The only certainty is that the ECB will keep its main key interest rate unchanged at 1%, its historic lowest since May 2009.
In fact, purchases of treasury bonds by the ECB doubled last week to almost EUR 1.35 billion.
And, according to indiscretions from market operators, it made new purchases of Irish and Portuguese securities yesterday.
Altogether, the central banks of the Eurosystem have purchased EUR 67 billion since May 2010.
Expectations of further purchases among market operators are of no doubt (see page 29).
Nor do economists expect a noticeable change of course.
While more than three-month-old loan operations have already disappeared from the ECB’s arsenal, the ECB still makes unlimited liquidity allowances on generous terms and for periods ranging from one week to three months.
Economists now expect this mechanism to be extended by a few months.
'I can't imagine that it's significantly changing its schedule.'
'Otherwise it would take the risk of provoking new tensions in the short term,' said Michael Schubert, an economist at Commerzbank.
'Profractual release announced in the first quarter of 2011 seems more likely,' Anticipates Norbert Brums, an economist at Sal.
Oppenheim.
With regard to its interventions in the public-debt market, "the ECB would have to maintain a purchase volume of these bonds in order to animate a shone market," adds Norbert.
In view of the importance of the exposures of European banks to the sovereign debt markets of the euro area, the issuing institution has no choice but to maintain its presence in the market.
'It has to replace the lack of investors in the market at all costs and to act in such a way as to prevent a runaway of the yields paid by the most fragile states,' a major bond manager testified yesterday.
If interest rates flew away, the Member States will be more embarrassed to reduce their idleness, if not stabilising it.
Safeguarding the euro is at that price.
In Italy, the Berlusconi government makes its back
After an alarming start of the week for Italy, the situation was somewhat relaxed yesterday with the turnover of 10-year-dollar bond rates.
A return to precarious calm.
At the end of a meeting with the Minister for the Economy, the regional presidents said they had heard Giulio Tremonti describe the situation as "indecipherable" and expect an "unprevisitible" weekend, which he later reselled.
In any case on Tuesday, the gap with German long rates had reached the peak of 210 basis points, and the price of hedging contracts against the risk of bankruptcy (CDS) skyrocketed at 263 percentage points, resulting in some affluence in Rome.
In the Council of Ministers, Silvio Berlusconi pointed out that in Spain the gap with German rates was greater than 400 basis points.
But his right-bodied man, Gianni Letta, said he was "very concerned" about "market turbulence" and possible contagion from Ireland.
"The good piece of evidence is that Italy, the third largest economy in the euro area, isn't like Greece or Ireland."
'The bad piece of news it's like Portugal,' explains Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
Simply put, its problem is the lack of growth and sluggish tax revenues that go with it, running the risk of no longer being able to meet its financing needs.
The latest forecasts from Brussels were a cold shower for the government.
Italian growth is now expected at 1.1% in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012, compared with the budget currently under consideration in the sneering of 1.3% and 2% respectively.
Under these circumstances, the European Commission calculates the general government deficit another 4.3% of GDP next year (compared with 3.9% predicted by the government) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
Hence the rumour of a new budget collective which could be put in place after the festivities for an amount of EUR 7 billion.
"If necessary, further austerity measures will have to be taken", said Economic Affairs Commissioner Olli Rehn.
For the time being, Italy receives support from international institutions.
'The fundamentals of its economy are in order and its accounts are good, we have been repeating it for a long time,' says Arrigo Sadun, the Italian trustee of the International Monetary Fund of Italy at the IMF.
'There is no problem in the immediate future, either in the short or in the long term, because, despite its large debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, NDLR), the stabilization efforts demanded of Italy are less than those demanded of other countries,' notes Pier Carlo Padoan, OECD Chief Economist.
There remains the issue of political instability.
On 14 December, Silvio Berlusconi will face a no-confidence motion in the Chamber of Deputies, as a result of which early elections could take place at the end of March.
At that time, it would open an unfavorable decision-making period in the event of a serious crisis.
Europeans reflect on the strengthening of anti-crisis schemes
Christine Lagarde hammered it again yesterday at a press conference in Paris: 'Europeans, I think it has been demonstrated again on Sunday during the day and during the night, are of course in solidarity, determined, committed to defending their currency and their monetary zone.'
Although quite relative, yesterday’s relaxation on European government bond rates brought water to the bulwark of governments and the ECB.
But we can't yet talk about an onset of normalization.
The credit rating agency Standard and Poor's has announced that it could decelerate Portugal's debt note, the target most targeted today by speculation.
'Like in May, there is a real lack of clarity and political visibility, which particularly affects banking systems,' Exane BNP Paribas economists say.
All the more because "politic time is much slower than market times".
Aware of this gap and its dangers, European countries are stepping up the discussions to strengthen existing anti-crisis arrangements.
The informal consultations between the finance ministers most directly concerned and, more broadly, with their G20 peers are well under way.
This is also in view of the meetings of the Euro-Coluntage and Ecofin at the beginning of next week.
The ECB is meeting today.
On the menu, the fate of non-conventional measures to support euro-area countries in markets (see above).
The European Financial Stability Facility (EFSF), its Executive Director, Klaus Regling, began an Asian tour in Singapore to inform Far Eastern Investors about the development of the crisis in Europe.
While the purpose of such visits is not to raise funds, it certainly paves the way for them.
The EFSF title issues are foreseen next month.
Yesterday, a U.S. official told the Reuters that the United States would support a possible increase in the funds mobilised by an additional direct commitment from the IMF.
The countries on the front lines, Portugal and Spain, for their part, are preparing to adopt structural reforms, particularly with regard to the organisation of the labour market.
Reforms in addition to tighter austerity measures.
Lisbon and Madrid want to take a different route from Greece and Ireland.
It is at all odds to avoid calling for international assistance before setting up their own fiscal corrections and deciding on the structural reforms needed to restore lasting confidence.
Finally, European lenders are discussing the options available to speeding up the process of standardising sovereign bond markets.
While in Paris on 24 November, Axel Weber first put forward the idea that if the EUR 750 billion mobilized by Europe and the International Monetary Fund were not sufficient, the Union would not hesitate to hand it over to the portfolio.
The economists of Barclays Capital, a major player in the European sovereign-debt market, believe that an increase of around EUR 100 billion would be “preferably” in the form of direct liquidity injection.
The European Financial Stabilisation Mechanism currently offers EUR 60 billion. The European Financial Stabilisation Mechanism.
