Avenging hackers and espouses
The WikiLeaks case has a double value.
On the one hand, it turns out to be an apparent scavenging scandal, a scandal which only appears in the context of the hypocrisy that governs relations between States, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it announces profound changes at the international level, foreshadows a future dominated by regress.
But let's do it by order.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is the confirmation of the fact that every file made up of a secret service (from whatever nation) consists exclusively of press clippings.
US “extraordinary revelations about Berlusconi’s sexual habits only resonate what for months” could be read in any newspaper (except those of which Berlusconi owns), and Qadhafi’s sinister caricunal profile has long been for cabaret performers.
The rule according to which secret bribes should only be composed of already known news is essential to the dynamics of secret services, and not only in this century.
If you go to a bookstore dedicated to esoteric publications, you will see that each book repeats (on the grail, the mystery of Rennes-le-Château, The Templiers or the Pink-Croix) exactly what had already been written in earlier works.
This not only because the author of occult texts does not like to do unpublished research (doesn't know where to look for news on the non-existent), but because those who dedicate themselves to occultism believe only in what they already know, and which reconfirms what they had already learned.
This is the mechanism of Dan Brown's success.
The same goes for secret folders.
The informant is smooth, slogged (or limited-spirit) the head of the secret services (otherwise he could, as I know, Editor at Libération), who only remembers as true what he acknowledges.
The top-secret information about Berlusconi that the US Embassy in Rome sent to the State Department was the same as those that Newsweek published the previous week.
So why did the revelations about these files make so much noise?
On the one hand, they say what anyone already known to know is that embassies, at least since the end of the Second World War, and since heads of State could telephone themselves or take an air to meet for dinner, have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small performance exercises, have turned into espionage centres.
Any spectator of investigative films knows this very well, and it is only out of hypocrisy that we pretend to ignore it.
However, repeating it publicly violates the duty of hypocrisy, and serves to put American diplomacy under bad light.
In the second place, the idea that any hacker can captivate the most secret secrets of the most powerful country in the world is a significant blow to the prestigration of the State Department.
As a result, the scandal does not involve the victims so much as the 'executors'.
But let's turn to the inspiring nature of what has happened.
For the time of Orwell, one could conceive of any power like a Big Brother that controlled every gesture of his subjects.
The Orwellian prophecy had been completely proven since, being able to control every movement through the telephone, every transaction carried out, the hotel visited, the motorway borrowed and so on, the citizen became the total victim of the eyes of power.
But when we demonstrate, as it happens now, that even the crypts of the secrets of power cannot escape the control of a hacker, the control report stops being unidirectional and becomes circular.
The power controls every citizen, but every citizen, or at least the hacker - elected as a vengear of the citizen - can know all the secrets of power.
How can a power that no longer has the opportunity to retain its own secrets?
Admittedly, Georg Simmel had already said, that a real secret is an blemsky (and an empty secret could never be unveiled); it is also true that everything to know about the character of Berlusconi or Merkel is indeed an empty secret, because of the public domain; but revealing, as WikiLeaks did, that Hillary Clinton’s secrets were empty secrets means to remove all power.
WikiLeaks didn’t wrong Sarkozy or Merkel, but turned it into too big at Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this wound inflicted on a very powerful power?
It is clear that in the near future, the Member States will no longer be able to upload any reserved information - this would be tantamount to publishing it on a poster glued at the corner.
But it is equally clear that, with current technologies, there is no reason to hope to be able to maintain confidential reports by telephone.
Nothing could be easilitative than to find out if and when a Head of State flew and contacted one of his colleagues.
How can private and reserved relations be maintained in the future?
I am well aware that, for the time being, my foresight is science-fiction and is therefore romantic, but I am obliged to imagine government agents who move discreetly in diligence to uncontrollable itineraries, carrying only messages learned by heart or, at the very most, by concealing the rare information written in the shoe of a shoot.
The information will be kept as a single copy in lock-off drawers: at the end of the Watergate espionage attempt was less successful than WikiLeaks.
I had the opportunity to write that technology is now progressing in crabs, i.e. hiding.
A century after the wireless telephoto revolutionized communications, the Internet re-established a wire telephotograph.
(Anological) videocass has enabled film researchers to explore a step-by-step film, moving forward and backward and unleasing all the secrets of the editing, while (digital CDs) now make it possible only to skip from chapter to chapter, i.e. by macroportions.
With high-degree high-fashion trains, we go from Rome to Milan in three o'clock in three and a half o'clock, while by airplane, and the movements it includes three-and-a-half hours.
It is therefore not an admiral that policy and communication techniques should belong to horse-drawn cars.
A final comment.
In the past, the press was trying to comprehend what was happening in the secrecy of embassies.
At the present time, it is the Embassies that request confidential information from the press.
Climate: how France shudders its financing promises
One year after the Copenhagen Accord, it is time to look at the fulfilment of the rapidly uncommitted pledges of financing (the so-called fast-start or early financing) made by the developed countries.
And the big French happhires are unveiled.
Under the Copenhagen Accord, the developed countries committed themselves to providing USD 30 billion between 2010 and 2012.
The European Union pledged EUR 7.2 billion over three years and EUR 1.26 billion (EUR 420 million per year).
At the opening of COP16, the EU pulled out the big game to convince its partners of the reality of its commitment to fast-start funding: report detailing the actions of its Member States, press conference, big side event, distribution of brochures...
The transparency operation was appreciable and valued: the developing countries and civil society would like all developed countries to do the same at the same level of accuracy.
The EU announces EUR 2.2 billion in 2010, which puts it in the right direction in order to fulfil its EUR 7.2 billion commitment in 2012.
But when we look in the detail, things are more complicated.
According to the "new and additional” Copenhagen agreement, this money must ensure that an additional effort is made without being to the detriment of official development assistance.
Unfortunately, there is no agreement on what "new and additional" means.
Although the European Commission had proposed harmonisation, some Member States whose procedure France had blocked the procedure.
Nowadays, everyone chooses the reference that suits them best.
The European European Commission has, for example, taken as a reference to 2009: international funding is being maintained, adding EUR 150 million over a three-year period for fast-start financing.
Other countries have chosen similar references in terms of year or level of official development assistance.
On the other hand, France estimates that the financing of "all new projects" for the year 2010 is "new and additional".
In other words, there is no reference to expenditure in previous years or at the overall level of official development assistance.
In concrete terms, the total amount of France’s support for developing countries can remain stable (or even decrease), it claims to respect its commitment to “new and additional” funding.
Another crucial element is the distribution between donations and loans.
Here again, there is no rule and everyone chooses its calculation method.
Some people, such as France, consider that EUR 1 lent is equivalent to EUR 1 given.
Of course, this is hardly justifiable: the effort is not comparable.
While the use of loans may be justified for certain investments that are profitable (often emission mitigation projects based on more efficient technologies), it is fallacilitating to present a euro which will be re-recorded as the equivalent of one euro that is given ... at most, it should not be counted under its commitments except the cost of the loan to the lender country (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France has nothing to do with such a reasoning: 'compensates' its commitment of EUR 420 million by making 91.5% loans (338.8 million) and 8.5% grants (EUR 36.1 million) - see the EU document for self-confertilisation.
It is the only country in the European Union to make such massive use of the loan.
46% in Germany, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia (in others) are fulfilling their commitments with 100% donations.
In other words, when the vast majority of the European Union puts one dollar to fulfil its commitment, France puts only 10 cents and considers that the effort is comparable.
Beautiful sweep.
To sum up, in its fast-developing financing, France is carrying out a double scam: on the one hand on the evolution of action over time and, on the other hand, on the reality of the financial effort.
By spending only EUR 36.1 million, it manages to make it believe that it spends 420 million "new and additional" in line with its promises.
Manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (because Bercy seems to have carried out the arbitrage) could have a double negative impact, both within the EU (while the other Member States would continue to give whether it is enough to lend?) and in the negotiations since the developing countries are not completely fooled...
The chapter on early financing is only a small, short-lived question compared to all climate discussions.
But it could be another grain of stove in the huge machinery of the UNFCCC.
Let us hope that this will not be the case.
In any other case, it is essential to draw lessons from this first year of early funding.
First of all, France could announce a change in its method of accounting for the years 2011 and 2012, in a spirit of respect for its commitments and fairness with the other countries of the European Union.
Above all, however, France could advance discussions on long-term financing issues (another crucial chapter currently under discussion): there will not elude the question of "additionality" or the way in which loans are credited.
France could change its position to become as Progressive on these challenges as it is about others.
Hope therefore rests with the new Minister of the Environment Kosciusko-Morizet who will arrive in Cancun next week.
A surprise announcement about France's fast start and its vision of "additionality" and the use of loans would increase the level of international confidence needed to move forward in the discussions.
Psyrigicide Alert Ingested by Children
A ten-year-old child ingests 81 different chemical substances in one day, according to a study published on Wednesday by Generations Futures (former Movement for Rights and Respect for Future Generations).
The association, which warns that its results 'have no significant statistical value with regard to the small number of samples analyzed, but are illustrating the problem dealt with', bought between July and September the foodstuffs generally consumed in four meals and one snack in one day by a ten-year-old child in the supermarkets of Oise and Paris.
Typical meals, including official recommendations - five fresh fruit and vegetables, three dairy products and 1.5 litres of water per day - as well as sweets, were examined by laboratories looking for chemical substances, pesticides, heavy metals and other pollutants.
The turnover of the fork is dripping: 81 chemical substances, of which 42 are classified as possible or probable carcinogens and five substances classified as carcinogens and 37 substances capable of acting as endocrine disruptors (PE), according to studies by several institutions such as the International Centre for Research on Cancer.
'Even if, in almost all cases, the legal limits for each chemical taken individually are not surpassed, our survey clearly shows that the reality of consumer exposure to possibly carcinogenic contaminants is a matter of serious concern,' notes the association.
For baking, butter and milk tea alone contain more than a dozen possible carcinogenic residues and three which are known as certain carcinogens as well as about 20 residues that could disturb the hormonal system.
Apple, imported from Brazil, planned as snack by the study, shows traces of six chemical substances, a fungicide still prohibited in France.
The same goes for Kenyan green beans, included in the luncheon, on which the study detected traces of a powerful edicide banned in the EU.
In addition, chopped steak steak, canned tuna, and even baguette of loaf and chewing gum were full of pesticides and other chemicals.
In tap water the analyses revealed the presence of nitrates and chloroform.
For overlending, salmon steak was "richest" with 34 chemical residues detected, and even an examination of the plastic dish used to reheat the microwave meal was not exempt.
The final risk for the consumer of this "contaminant cocktail" "is probably underestimated", according to the study.
For its part, the European Ecology-Greens/Greens/Alliance Alliance has proposed 'completing the urgency of the situation' to introduce a Moratorium on the use of pesticides, in order to stop 'the increase in diseases of civilisation'.
In July, a study by the European Agency for Food Safety (EFSA) reported residues of 365 different pesticides identified in fruit and vegetables consumed in Europe, 76 of them in cereals.
